var e=`Teaching Pokémon video-game facts for Pulse. Launch MSRP only when documented. No used-market prices, no eBay solds, no TCG street dates as game launches.`,t=`2026-09-16`,n=314,r=[{id:`game-red-green-1996`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Red and Green launched in Japan on February 27, 1996`,stat:`1996`,sub:`Game Boy. That date is Pokémon Day.`,why:`Pocket Monsters Red and Green shipped in Japan on February 27, 1996, on Game Boy. Game Freak made them. The rest of the world did not get a cartridge that day.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gb`,`japan`]},{id:`game-pokemon-day`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Day is February 27 because Red and Green launched that day`,stat:`2/27`,sub:`1996 in Japan. The West got Red and Blue in 1998.`,why:`February 27 is Pokémon Day because Red and Green launched in Japan that day in 1996. The West received Red and Blue in 1998. Same franchise start, later street date.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gb`]},{id:`game-red-blue-west`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The West received Red and Blue on September 28, 1998`,stat:`1998`,sub:`Game Boy. Two years after Japan.`,why:`Nintendo of America launched Pokémon Red and Blue on September 28, 1998. They are the Kanto pair the West grew up on. They are not the 2023 TCG set named 151.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gb`]},{id:`game-green-japan-only`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Green never shipped as a Western cartridge`,stat:`JP`,sub:`Western Blue stands in for Green's role.`,why:`Pocket Monsters Green stayed a Japan Game Boy SKU. Western Blue filled that half of the pair, with Blastoise on the box. Do not catalog a US Green cart from 1998.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gb`,`japan`]},{id:`game-blue-japan`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Japan's Blue was a later 1996 special edition, not Western Blue`,stat:`1996`,sub:`October 15 in Japan. Different encounters than Red and Green.`,why:`Pocket Monsters Blue launched in Japan on October 15, 1996, as a special edition with its own encounter table. Western Blue is a different localization of the pair. Do not treat the two Blues as one SKU.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gb`,`japan`]},{id:`game-yellow-follower`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Yellow put Pikachu on screen behind you`,stat:`GB`,sub:`Japan 1998, West 1999. Let's Go did the partner trick on Switch.`,why:`Pokémon Yellow launched in Japan on September 12, 1998, and in North America on October 18, 1999. Pikachu follows the player. Let's Go Pikachu and Let's Go Eevee reused a partner-on-screen idea on Switch in 2018.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gb`,`pikachu`]},{id:`game-yellow-anime`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Yellow is the Kanto game shaped by the anime`,stat:`1998`,sub:`Starter is Pikachu. Rival takes Eevee.`,why:`Yellow follows the anime more than Red and Blue do: you start with Pikachu, and the rival takes Eevee. It is still a Game Boy Kanto game. It is not a cartoon episode.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gb`,`pikachu`,`eevee`]},{id:`game-kanto-151`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The original Kanto Pokédex has 151 entries`,stat:`151`,sub:`Mew is 151. The TCG set 151 is a 2023 paper joke.`,why:`Red, Green, Blue, Yellow, FireRed, and LeafGreen use a 151-entry Kanto Pokédex. Mew is number 151. The 2023 English TCG expansion named 151 is named after that list. The cards are new printings.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`dex`]},{id:`game-mew-151`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Mew was the secret 151st Pokémon, not a wild spawn`,stat:`#151`,sub:`Event or glitch in Gen 1. Not a route encounter.`,why:`Mew is National Dex #151. In Generation I you could not find it as a normal wild Pokémon. Players used events or the famous glitch. It is a Mythical, not a box legendary.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`mew`,`dex`]},{id:`game-missingno`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`MissingNo is a Gen 1 glitch, not a Pokédex species`,stat:`'M`,sub:`Red and Blue. It cloned the sixth bag item.`,why:`MissingNo is a programming leftover in Pokémon Red and Blue, not a real Pokédex entry. Players used it to duplicate the sixth item. Later games do not include it as a catchable monster.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gb`]},{id:`game-gold-silver`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Gold and Silver launched in Japan on November 21, 1999`,stat:`GBC`,sub:`North America: October 15, 2000. Johto plus a return to Kanto.`,why:`Pokémon Gold and Silver launched in Japan on November 21, 1999, on Game Boy Color, and in North America on October 15, 2000. You beat Johto, then travel Kanto. That second region is still the game, not a DLC pack.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`,`kanto`]},{id:`game-johto-100`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Johto added 100 new Pokémon`,stat:`100`,sub:`Chikorita through Celebi. National Dex hit 251.`,why:`Gold and Silver introduced 100 new species, numbers 152 through 251. Celebi is 251. The Johto regional list in those games also includes Kanto Pokémon you meet along the way.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`dex`,`gbc`]},{id:`game-national-251`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`After Gold and Silver the National Dex was 251`,stat:`251`,sub:`151 from Kanto plus 100 from Johto.`,why:`Generation II closed at 251 National Dex entries. That number is a game census, not a TCG set size. Crystal did not add a new species on top of Gold and Silver.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`dex`]},{id:`game-crystal-kris`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Crystal let you play as a girl named Kris`,stat:`2000`,sub:`First girl trainer in a main-series game. Gold and Silver were boy-only.`,why:`Pokémon Crystal, Game Boy Color, launched in Japan on December 14, 2000, and in North America on July 29, 2001. It is the first main-series game with a female player character, Kris. Gold and Silver only offered the boy.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`,`crystal`]},{id:`game-crystal-suicune-box`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Crystal's English box shows Suicune`,stat:`BOX`,sub:`Suicune is on the front. Kris is the trainer named on the back.`,why:`English Pokémon Crystal puts Suicune on the box art. Kris is the trainer you can play, named on the back of the box. Cover art and playable trainer are two different things.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`,`crystal`]},{id:`game-gold-hooh-box`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Gold's box is Ho-Oh. Silver's box is Lugia`,stat:`BOX`,sub:`Version mascots, not the player characters.`,why:`Gold and Silver put Ho-Oh and Lugia on the boxes, and Crystal puts Suicune there. Those are cover monsters. Ethan and Kris are the trainers you play.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`]},{id:`game-time-capsule`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Time Capsule moved Gen 1 Pokémon into Gold and Silver`,stat:`GBC`,sub:`Link-cable trade back to Red, Blue, and Yellow.`,why:`Gold, Silver, and Crystal can trade with Red, Blue, and Yellow through the Time Capsule. Only Generation I species can go backward. New Johto monsters stay in Gen 2.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`kanto`,`gbc`,`gb`]},{id:`game-day-night`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Gold and Silver added a clock and a day-night cycle`,stat:`CLOCK`,sub:`Some Pokémon and evolutions care what time it is.`,why:`Generation II tracks time of day. Certain wild encounters change, and Eevee's friendship evolutions split by day and night. Crystal also used the Game Boy Color infrared port for mystery gifts.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`]},{id:`game-breeding`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Breeding started in Gold and Silver at the Day Care`,stat:`EGG`,sub:`Two parents. An egg. Not a TCG pack mechanic.`,why:`The Day Care in Gold and Silver lets two compatible Pokémon produce an egg. That is how baby Pokémon and later the Masuda method exist. It is a game loop, not a pack-opening trick.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`]},{id:`game-held-items`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Held items arrived in Generation II`,stat:`HOLD`,sub:`A Pokémon can carry one item into battle.`,why:`Gold and Silver let a Pokémon hold one item. Leftovers, berries, and evolution items all use that slot. Generation I had no held-item system.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`]},{id:`game-gender`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Gold and Silver gave most Pokémon a gender`,stat:`♂♀`,sub:`Needed for breeding. Generation I had no gender flag.`,why:`Generation II added male and female ratios. Breeding depends on that flag. Nidoran already showed two forms in Gen 1, but most species were ungendered until Gold and Silver.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`]},{id:`game-dark-steel`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Dark and Steel types debuted in Gold and Silver`,stat:`17`,sub:`Generation I had 15 types. These two made 17.`,why:`Gold and Silver introduced Dark and Steel. Psychic lost its Gen 1 dominance because Dark resists it and Steel resists Poison. Fairy did not exist yet. That comes in X and Y.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`]},{id:`game-friendship`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Friendship as an evolution stat started in Generation II`,stat:`HAPPY`,sub:`Walk, battle, no fainting. Then some species evolve.`,why:`Gold and Silver track how much a Pokémon likes you. Golbat becomes Crobat, Chansey becomes Blissey, and Eevee becomes Espeon or Umbreon depending on the clock, with no evolutionary stone.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`]},{id:`game-umbreon-espeon`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Umbreon needs night friendship. Espeon needs day friendship`,stat:`NIGHT`,sub:`Same happiness. Opposite clock. No Moon Stone.`,why:`In Gold and Silver, Eevee with high friendship evolves into Espeon during the day and Umbreon at night. There is no Moon Stone on that line. A TCG Umbreon does not care what time it is.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`umbreon`,`espeon`,`gbc`]},{id:`game-ruby-sapphire`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ruby and Sapphire launched in Japan on November 21, 2002`,stat:`GBA`,sub:`North America: March 19, 2003. Hoenn. Game Boy Advance.`,why:`Pokémon Ruby and Sapphire launched in Japan on November 21, 2002, and in North America on March 19, 2003, on Game Boy Advance. They open the Hoenn region. Abilities and natures start here.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`gba`]},{id:`game-hoenn-202`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The original Hoenn Pokédex has 202 entries`,stat:`202`,sub:`Treecko through Deoxys in Ruby, Sapphire, and Emerald.`,why:`Ruby, Sapphire, and Emerald use a 202-entry Hoenn Pokédex ending at Deoxys. Omega Ruby and Alpha Sapphire later stretch that regional list to 211. Two Hoenn dex sizes, same region.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`dex`,`gba`]},{id:`game-hoenn-135-new`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Hoenn added 135 new Pokémon`,stat:`135`,sub:`Treecko through Deoxys. National Dex hit 386.`,why:`Generation III introduced 135 new species, numbers 252 through 386. Deoxys is 386. That census is the games, not a TCG block named after Hoenn years later.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`dex`]},{id:`game-national-386`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`After Ruby and Sapphire the National Dex was 386`,stat:`386`,sub:`251 from Gen 2 plus 135 Hoenn species.`,why:`Generation III closed the National Dex at 386. FireRed, LeafGreen, and Emerald share that ceiling. Platinum-era numbers come later.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`dex`]},{id:`game-ruby-groudon`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ruby's box is Groudon. Sapphire's box is Kyogre`,stat:`BOX`,sub:`May and Brendan are the trainers, not the covers.`,why:`Ruby features Groudon, Sapphire features Kyogre, and Emerald features Rayquaza. May is the girl you can play. Cover legendary and playable trainer are not the same thing.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`gba`]},{id:`game-may-rs`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`May is the Hoenn girl. She is not Groudon`,stat:`MAY`,sub:`Ruby and Sapphire let you pick May or Brendan.`,why:`Ruby and Sapphire, 2002–2003, introduced a female player option in a paired version set: May. Brendan is the boy. The boxes still show the version legendaries.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`gba`]},{id:`game-abilities`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Abilities started in Ruby and Sapphire`,stat:`ABILITY`,sub:`Each species gets a battle trait. Generation I and II had none.`,why:`Generation III gave every Pokémon an Ability — Intimidate, Levitate, Swift Swim, and the rest. Hidden Abilities come later. This is a game rule, not a card ability from the TCG.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`gba`]},{id:`game-natures`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Natures started in Ruby and Sapphire and tilt two stats`,stat:`NATURE`,sub:`Plus 10 percent to one stat, minus 10 percent to another.`,why:`A Nature raises one stat by 10 percent and lowers another, except the five neutral natures. Ruby and Sapphire introduced them. They still matter in competitive play.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`gba`]},{id:`game-emerald`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Emerald is the third Hoenn game, with Rayquaza on the box`,stat:`2004`,sub:`Japan September 16, 2004. North America May 1, 2005.`,why:`Pokémon Emerald launched in Japan on September 16, 2004, and in North America on May 1, 2005. Rayquaza is the cover legendary. It adds the Battle Frontier and a combined Team Magma and Team Aqua plot.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`gba`]},{id:`game-battle-frontier-emerald`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Emerald's Battle Frontier is post-game facilities, not a new region`,stat:`FRONTIER`,sub:`Battle Tower plus six more. Platinum built another later.`,why:`After the Hoenn league, Emerald opens the Battle Frontier: several battle facilities with set rules. It is post-game, not a second map like Kanto in Gold and Silver. Platinum later built a Sinnoh Frontier.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`gba`,`play`]},{id:`game-frlg-gba`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`FireRed and LeafGreen are the 2004 Game Boy Advance Kanto remakes`,stat:`2004`,sub:`Japan January 29. North America September 7. Not the 2026 eShop ports.`,why:`Pokémon FireRed and LeafGreen launched in Japan on January 29, 2004, and in North America on September 7, 2004, on Game Boy Advance. They remake Red and Blue with Gen 3 mechanics. The Switch eShop copies are a 2026 rerelease of these remakes.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gba`,`frlg`]},{id:`game-frlg-sevii`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`FireRed and LeafGreen add the Sevii Islands after the league`,stat:`SEVII`,sub:`Post-game islands. More Pokémon than original Red and Blue.`,why:`After the Indigo League, FireRed and LeafGreen open the Sevii Islands. That post-game is part of the GBA remakes and of the 2026 Switch ports. Original Red and Blue had no Sevii map.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gba`,`frlg`]},{id:`game-leaf-frlg`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The FireRed and LeafGreen girl was later named Leaf`,stat:`LEAF`,sub:`Red is the boy. The GBA boxes still show Charizard and Venusaur.`,why:`FireRed and LeafGreen let you pick a girl trainer, later called Leaf in official media, while Red is the boy. FireRed's box is Charizard and LeafGreen's is Venusaur, so the cover monster and the player are different.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gba`,`frlg`]},{id:`game-frlg-switch-2026`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`FireRed and LeafGreen hit the Switch eShop on Pokémon Day 2026`,stat:`2/27`,sub:`February 27, 2026. Same Kanto, Sevii Islands included.`,why:`The Game Boy Advance Kanto remakes returned as paid Nintendo Switch and Switch 2 eShop games on February 27, 2026, Pokémon Day. Sevii Islands are in. This is a video-game rerelease, not the paper 30th Celebration TCG set.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`switch`,`switch2`,`frlg`],asOf:`2026-02-27`},{id:`game-frlg-switch-price`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Switch FireRed and LeafGreen ports launched at $19.99 each`,stat:`$19.99`,sub:`Standalone eShop. You buy one version, not a bundle.`,why:`Nintendo listed Pokémon FireRed Version and Pokémon LeafGreen Version at $19.99 USD each on the eShop in 2026. That is launch MSRP for the ports. It is not a used-cart price and not a booster-box number.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`switch`,`frlg`],asOf:`2026-02-27`},{id:`game-frlg-not-nso`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Switch FireRed and LeafGreen are not Nintendo Switch Online classics`,stat:`eShop`,sub:`Paid downloads. No NSO membership required to buy or play.`,why:`Nintendo sold FireRed and LeafGreen as standalone eShop games, not as Game Boy Advance – Nintendo Classics titles. Online play is not supported. Local wireless still works, like the GBA Wireless Club.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`switch`,`frlg`]},{id:`game-home-frlg-2026`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon HOME is scheduled to link Switch FireRed and LeafGreen in October 2026`,stat:`HOME`,sub:`Complete the Kanto dex in HOME's mobile app for a Celebi.`,why:`Nintendo announced Pokémon HOME support for the Switch FireRed and LeafGreen ports beginning October 2026. Completing the Kanto Pokédex can grant a Celebi in the mobile HOME app. That is a transfer plan, not a TCG promo.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`switch`,`frlg`,`home`],asOf:`2026-10-01`},{id:`game-wireless-frlg`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`FireRed and LeafGreen replaced the link cable with a wireless adapter`,stat:`WIRELESS`,sub:`Union Room. Up to five players locally on GBA.`,why:`The GBA Wireless Adapter let FireRed and LeafGreen trade and battle without a cable, in the Union Room. The 2026 Switch ports keep local wireless and still do not add online trades.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gba`,`frlg`]},{id:`game-diamond-pearl`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Diamond and Pearl launched in Japan on September 28, 2006`,stat:`DS`,sub:`North America: April 22, 2007. Sinnoh. Dual-screen Nintendo DS.`,why:`Pokémon Diamond and Pearl launched in Japan on September 28, 2006, and in North America on April 22, 2007, on Nintendo DS. They open Sinnoh. The physical/special split starts here.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`ds`]},{id:`game-sinnoh-151`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Diamond and Pearl's Sinnoh Pokédex has 151 entries`,stat:`151`,sub:`Same count as Kanto, different list. Ends at Manaphy.`,why:`The original Sinnoh Pokédex in Diamond, Pearl, Brilliant Diamond, and Shining Pearl has 151 entries, from Turtwig to Manaphy. That is a regional list size, not the Kanto 151. Platinum later expands it.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`dex`,`ds`]},{id:`game-sinnoh-107-new`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sinnoh added 107 new Pokémon`,stat:`107`,sub:`Turtwig through Arceus. National Dex hit 493.`,why:`Generation IV introduced 107 new species, numbers 387 through 493. Arceus is 493. Mythicals in that range were still event Pokémon in the DS games.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`dex`]},{id:`game-national-493`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`After Diamond and Pearl the National Dex was 493`,stat:`493`,sub:`386 from Gen 3 plus 107 Sinnoh species.`,why:`Generation IV closed the National Dex at 493. HeartGold and SoulSilver share that ceiling. Unova numbers start at 494.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`dex`]},{id:`game-physical-special`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Diamond and Pearl split physical and special by the move, not the type`,stat:`SPLIT`,sub:`Thunderbolt is special. Thunder Punch is physical. Same type.`,why:`Before Gen 4, a type was wholly physical or special. Diamond and Pearl assign that to each move, so Thunderbolt stays special and Thunder Punch becomes physical. Competitive teams changed overnight.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`ds`,`play`]},{id:`game-platinum`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Platinum is the third Sinnoh game, with Giratina on the box`,stat:`2008`,sub:`Japan September 13, 2008. North America March 22, 2009.`,why:`Pokémon Platinum launched in Japan on September 13, 2008, and in North America on March 22, 2009. Giratina is the cover legendary. The Distortion World is the new map. It is an enhanced Sinnoh, not a remake of a remake.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`ds`]},{id:`game-platinum-dex-210`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Platinum's Sinnoh Pokédex grows to 210 entries`,stat:`210`,sub:`Giratina is 210 on that list. Diamond and Pearl stayed at 151.`,why:`Platinum expands the Sinnoh Pokédex from 151 to 210, ending at Giratina. Brilliant Diamond and Shining Pearl went back to the 151 Diamond and Pearl list. Same region, two dex sizes.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`dex`,`ds`]},{id:`game-dawn-dpp`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Dawn is the Sinnoh girl. Dialga and Palkia are the boxes`,stat:`DAWN`,sub:`Lucas is the boy. Cover legends are not the player.`,why:`Diamond, Pearl, and Platinum let you play as Dawn or Lucas. Diamond's box is Dialga, Pearl's is Palkia, and Platinum's is Giratina. The girl on the title screen is not the legendary on the spine.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`ds`]},{id:`game-hgss`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`HeartGold and SoulSilver remade Gold and Silver on DS in 2009`,stat:`DS`,sub:`Japan September 12, 2009. North America March 14, 2010.`,why:`Pokémon HeartGold and SoulSilver launched in Japan on September 12, 2009, and in North America on March 14, 2010. They remake Johto and the Kanto return with following Pokémon and the Pokéwalker. They are not a TCG subset.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`kanto`,`ds`]},{id:`game-hgss-follow`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`HeartGold and SoulSilver let one Pokémon walk behind you`,stat:`FOLLOW`,sub:`Overworld partner. Yellow did Pikachu. This did almost any species.`,why:`HGSS put a party Pokémon on screen in the overworld. Yellow only followed with Pikachu. Legends Arceus and later games reused following in different ways. It is a field feature, not a battle gimmick.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`ds`]},{id:`game-hgss-lyra`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Lyra is the HeartGold and SoulSilver girl. The boxes are Ho-Oh and Lugia`,stat:`LYRA`,sub:`Ethan is the boy. Same cover legends as Gold and Silver.`,why:`HeartGold's box is Ho-Oh and SoulSilver's box is Lugia. Lyra is the girl trainer and Ethan is the boy. Crystal already had Kris, so different games can share Johto without sharing the same girl.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`ds`]},{id:`game-pokewalker`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`HeartGold and SoulSilver packed a Pokéwalker pedometer`,stat:`WALK`,sub:`Infrared. Steps unlock routes and items. Not an app.`,why:`The Pokéwalker is a clip-on pedometer that came with HeartGold and SoulSilver. You transfer one Pokémon onto it, walk, and unlock areas. It talks to the DS over infrared. It is not Pokémon GO.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`ds`]},{id:`game-black-white`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Black and White launched in Japan on September 18, 2010`,stat:`DS`,sub:`North America: March 6, 2011. Unova. New species only at first.`,why:`Pokémon Black and White launched in Japan on September 18, 2010, and in North America on March 6, 2011, still on DS. Unova's story dex is almost all new Pokémon until the post-game National Dex.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`ds`]},{id:`game-unova-156`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Unova added 156 new Pokémon`,stat:`156`,sub:`Victini through Genesect. National Dex hit 649.`,why:`Generation V introduced 156 new species, numbers 494 through 649. Victini is 494. Genesect is 649. That is the largest single-generation species dump in the main series.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`dex`]},{id:`game-bw-fresh-dex`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Black and White's Unova Pokédex is 155 numbered entries plus Victini`,stat:`155`,sub:`Victini is #000. The story routes hide older generations.`,why:`The Unova Pokédex in Black and White runs #000 Victini through #155 Genesect. Until you unlock the National Dex, wild Unova is new species. That was the design: a second first adventure.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`dex`,`ds`]},{id:`game-national-649`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`After Black and White the National Dex was 649`,stat:`649`,sub:`493 from Gen 4 plus 156 Unova species.`,why:`Generation V closed the National Dex at 649. Black 2 and White 2 share that ceiling and add more Unova-regional slots, not more National numbers.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`dex`]},{id:`game-hilda-bw`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Hilda is the Black and White girl. The boxes are Reshiram and Zekrom`,stat:`HILDA`,sub:`Hilbert is the boy. Cover dragons are not the player.`,why:`Black's box is Reshiram and White's box is Zekrom. Hilda and Hilbert are the trainers, and Kyurem waits in the story.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`ds`]},{id:`game-bw-seasons`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Black and White change the overworld with the real-world season`,stat:`SEASON`,sub:`DS clock. Deerling and Sawsbuck shift form.`,why:`Unova tracks spring, summer, autumn, and winter from the DS clock. Routes look different. Deerling and Sawsbuck change form. It is a field system, not a TCG mechanic.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`ds`]},{id:`game-b2w2-sequel`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Black 2 and White 2 are sequels two years later, not remakes`,stat:`2012`,sub:`Japan June 23, 2012. North America October 7, 2012.`,why:`Pokémon Black 2 and White 2 launched in Japan on June 23, 2012, and in North America on October 7, 2012. They are sequels set two years after Black and White, with new towns and a different Unova layout. They are not Gold-and-Silver-style remakes.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`ds`]},{id:`game-b2w2-dex-300`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Black 2 and White 2 stretch the Unova Pokédex to 300 entries`,stat:`300`,sub:`Older species return. National Dex is still 649.`,why:`The Unova Pokédex in Black 2 and White 2 runs to 300, ending at Genesect. Returning Kanto-through-Sinnoh species fill the extra slots. The National Dex does not grow past 649.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`dex`,`ds`]},{id:`game-rosa-b2w2`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Rosa is the Black 2 and White 2 girl. Nate is the boy`,stat:`ROSA`,sub:`New player pair. Same Unova, later story.`,why:`Black 2 and White 2 replace Hilda and Hilbert with Rosa and Nate. The boxes swap the dragons relative to Black and White: Black 2 features Zekrom, White 2 features Reshiram. Still not the girl on the cover as the legend.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`ds`]},{id:`game-pwt`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Black 2 and White 2 add the Pokémon World Tournament`,stat:`PWT`,sub:`Gym Leaders and Champions from older games, as NPC fights.`,why:`The Pokémon World Tournament in Black 2 and White 2 lets you battle classic Gym Leaders and Champions under set formats. It is an in-game facility. It is not the real-world VGC circuit.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`ds`,`play`]},{id:`game-hidden-ability`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Hidden Abilities arrived in Generation V`,stat:`HA`,sub:`Dream World first, then later Hidden Ability hunts.`,why:`Black and White introduced Hidden Abilities, originally through the online Pokémon Dream World. They are a third ability slot some species can have. The TCG does not use this term the same way.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`ds`]},{id:`game-shiny-charm`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Shiny Charm started as a Black 2 and White 2 National Dex reward`,stat:`CHARM`,sub:`Key Item. Stacks with other shiny odds. Not a card.`,why:`Complete the National Dex in Black 2 or White 2 and the game gives you the Shiny Charm, which raises shiny odds. Later games keep a charm. It is a Key Item, not a TCG chase.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`shiny`,`ds`]},{id:`game-xy-2013`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`X and Y launched worldwide on October 12, 2013`,stat:`3DS`,sub:`First main-series pair on Nintendo 3DS. Kalos. Mega Evolution.`,why:`Pokémon X and Y launched on the same day worldwide, October 12, 2013, on Nintendo 3DS. They open Kalos. Mega Evolution, the Fairy type, and the 1-in-4,096 shiny rate start here.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`3ds`,`mega`]},{id:`game-xy-simultaneous`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`X and Y were the first main-series games with a worldwide same-day launch`,stat:`2013`,sub:`No two-year West wait. 3DS.`,why:`Before X and Y, the West usually waited months or years after Japan. October 12, 2013, was simultaneous. Later main games kept that pattern. TCG English sets still often trail Japan.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-kalos-72`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Kalos added 72 new Pokémon`,stat:`72`,sub:`Chespin through Volcanion. National Dex hit 721.`,why:`Generation VI introduced 72 new species, numbers 650 through 721. Volcanion is 721. That is the smallest new-species count of any generation through Paldea.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`dex`]},{id:`game-national-721`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`After X and Y the National Dex was 721`,stat:`721`,sub:`649 from Unova plus 72 Kalos species.`,why:`Generation VI closed the National Dex at 721. Omega Ruby and Alpha Sapphire share that ceiling. Alola numbers start at 722.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`dex`]},{id:`game-kalos-three-dex`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Kalos splits the regional Pokédex into Central, Coastal, and Mountain lists`,stat:`3 DEX`,sub:`Three area lists. Not one numbered Johto-style dex.`,why:`X and Y do not use a single 1-to-N Kalos dex the way Kanto did. They split encounters across Central, Coastal, and Mountain Pokédexes. Completing all three is the regional hunt.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`dex`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-mega-xy`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Mega Evolution started in X and Y in 2013`,stat:`2013`,sub:`Kalos. A Key Stone plus a Mega Stone. One battle at a time.`,why:`Mega Evolution first appeared in Pokémon X and Y in 2013. A trainer's Key Stone and a Pokémon's Mega Stone trigger a temporary battle form. Legends Z-A brought Mega Evolution back as a main-series gimmick in 2025.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`3ds`,`mega`]},{id:`game-fairy-type`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Fairy type debuted in X and Y`,stat:`FAIRY`,sub:`Eighteenth type. Dragons finally have a bad matchup.`,why:`X and Y added Fairy as the 18th type. Dragon is weak to it. Steel lost its resistances to Dark and Ghost in the same generation. Sylveon is the Fairy Eeveelution from this pair.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`3ds`,`sylveon`]},{id:`game-types-18`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`There have been 18 types since X and Y`,stat:`18`,sub:`Fifteen in Gen 1. Seventeen after Dark and Steel. Eighteen with Fairy.`,why:`Generation I had 15 types. Gold and Silver added Dark and Steel for 17. X and Y added Fairy for 18. No new type has been added in Galar or Paldea.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-serena-xy`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Serena is the Kalos girl. The boxes are Xerneas and Yveltal`,stat:`SERENA`,sub:`Calem is the boy. Zygarde is the third Kalos legend.`,why:`X's box is Xerneas and Y's box is Yveltal. Serena and Calem are the trainers, with Zygarde as the third member of that trio.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-oras`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Omega Ruby and Alpha Sapphire remade Hoenn on 3DS in 2014`,stat:`2014`,sub:`Worldwide November 21, 2014. Primal Reversion plus more Megas.`,why:`Pokémon Omega Ruby and Alpha Sapphire launched worldwide on November 21, 2014. They remake Ruby and Sapphire with Mega Evolution and Primal Groudon and Primal Kyogre. They are not Emerald remakes.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`3ds`,`mega`]},{id:`game-primal`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Primal Reversion is Omega Ruby and Alpha Sapphire's Groudon and Kyogre form`,stat:`PRIMAL`,sub:`No Mega Stone. The Red and Blue Orbs. Weather on the field.`,why:`Primal Groudon and Primal Kyogre are ORAS-only battle forms, triggered by the Red Orb and Blue Orb, not Mega Stones. They lock harsh sunlight or heavy rain. Mega Rayquaza is a separate ORAS Mega that needs no stone.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`3ds`,`mega`]},{id:`game-oras-dex-211`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Omega Ruby and Alpha Sapphire's Hoenn Pokédex has 211 entries`,stat:`211`,sub:`Nine more than the original 202. Still ends at Deoxys.`,why:`ORAS expands the Hoenn Pokédex from 202 to 211. The extra slots are mostly returning species available in Hoenn on 3DS. National Dex remains 721.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`dex`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-exp-share-xy`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`X and Y turned Exp. Share into a Key Item that feeds the whole party`,stat:`EXP`,sub:`No more held item. Later games kept a version of this.`,why:`In X and Y the Exp Share is a Key Item, and when it is on every party member gains experience. Older games made it a held item for one Pokémon.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-sun-moon`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sun and Moon launched worldwide on November 18, 2016`,stat:`3DS`,sub:`Alola. Z-moves. First regional forms. No traditional gyms.`,why:`Pokémon Sun and Moon launched worldwide on November 18, 2016, on 3DS. Alola uses Trials and Grand Trials instead of eight gyms. Z-moves and Alolan forms start here.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-alola-302`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sun and Moon's Alola Pokédex has 302 entries`,stat:`302`,sub:`Rowlet through Marshadow. Ultra Sun and Ultra Moon later hit 403.`,why:`The original Alola Pokédex in Sun and Moon has 302 entries. Island dexes (Melemele, Akala, Ula'ula, Poni) are subsets of that list. Ultra Sun and Ultra Moon expand the full Alola list to 403.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`dex`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-alola-88`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Alola added 88 new Pokémon`,stat:`88`,sub:`Rowlet through Magearna, then Ultra Beasts and Meltan later.`,why:`Generation VII introduced 88 new National Dex species through Melmetal, numbers 722 through 809. Meltan and Melmetal came from Pokémon GO in 2018, after Sun and Moon shipped.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`dex`,`go`]},{id:`game-z-move`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Z-moves are Alola's once-per-battle super moves`,stat:`Z`,sub:`Z-Crystal plus Z-Ring. Sun and Moon, 2016. Not Dynamax.`,why:`Z-moves debuted in Pokémon Sun and Moon in 2016. A Z-Crystal and Z-Ring let one Pokémon use one powered-up move per battle. They are not Mega Evolution, not Dynamax, and not Terastal.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-alolan-forms`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Alolan forms were the first regional variants`,stat:`ALOLA`,sub:`Same dex number, new typing. Vulpix, Meowth, Raichu, and more.`,why:`Sun and Moon introduced regional forms: Alolan Vulpix is Ice, Alolan Meowth is Dark, Alolan Raichu is Electric/Psychic. Later games added Galarian, Hisuian, and Paldean forms. Same National number, different Pokémon.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-ride-alola`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sun and Moon replaced HMs with Ride Pokémon`,stat:`RIDE`,sub:`Tauros, Lapras, Charizard. You do not teach Cut to a party member.`,why:`Alola's Ride Pager calls a Pokémon to smash rocks, surf, or fly. The party no longer wastes moveslots on Hidden Machines for field puzzles. Galar later removed HMs in a different way.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-totem`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Totem Pokémon are the Alola trial bosses`,stat:`TOTEM`,sub:`Bigger model. Aura boost. Allies can be called in.`,why:`Each Alola trial ends in a Totem battle: a powered-up wild Pokémon that can call allies. They are not gym leaders and not Ultra Beasts. Totem is a Sun and Moon (and USUM) field gimmick.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-ultra-beasts`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ultra Beasts are extradimensional species from Ultra Space`,stat:`UB`,sub:`Nihilego first. Beast Balls catch them better.`,why:`Sun and Moon introduce Ultra Beasts as invaders from Ultra Space. Beast Balls work better on them than on ordinary Pokémon. They have National Dex numbers. They are not Mythicals in the event-only sense.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-selene-sm`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Alola girl is later called Selene. The boxes are Solgaleo and Lunala`,stat:`SELENE`,sub:`Elio is the boy. Cover legends are the sun and moon lions.`,why:`Sun's box is Solgaleo. Moon's box is Lunala. The girl trainer is called Selene in later official media. Necrozma is the third of that light trio, especially in Ultra Sun and Ultra Moon.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-usum`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ultra Sun and Ultra Moon are 2017 enhanced Alola games, not remakes`,stat:`2017`,sub:`Worldwide November 17, 2017. New episodes. Same 3DS region.`,why:`Pokémon Ultra Sun and Ultra Moon launched worldwide on November 17, 2017. They are enhanced versions of Sun and Moon with new story beats and Ultra Space visits, not third-version remakes of a remake.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-usum-403`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ultra Sun and Ultra Moon's Alola Pokédex has 403 entries`,stat:`403`,sub:`One hundred one more than Sun and Moon's 302.`,why:`USUM expands the Alola Pokédex from 302 to 403. Returning species and more Ultra Beasts fill the extra slots. National Dex after Meltan and Melmetal reaches 809.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`dex`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-usum-ultra-space`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ultra Sun and Ultra Moon let you travel Ultra Space more freely`,stat:`WORMHOLE`,sub:`Ultra Wormholes. Legendary hunts. Not a new region on the map.`,why:`USUM adds more Ultra Wormhole travel, where you can encounter legendary Pokémon and Ultra Beasts. It is still Alola plus other dimensions, not a Johto-style second region.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-national-809`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`After Meltan and Melmetal the National Dex was 809`,stat:`809`,sub:`Alola's 88 new species, including the GO Mythicals.`,why:`Meltan is 808 and Melmetal is 809. They arrived through Pokémon GO in 2018 and Let's Go. Generation VII's National Dex closes there. Galar starts at 810.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`dex`,`go`]},{id:`game-lgpe`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Let's Go Pikachu and Let's Go Eevee put Kanto on Switch in 2018`,stat:`2018`,sub:`November 16. Followers. GO-style catching. $59.99 launch MSRP.`,why:`Let's Go Pikachu and Let's Go Eevee launched November 16, 2018, on Nintendo Switch at $59.99 USD. They remake Yellow-style Kanto with overworld catching. Battles stay turn-based.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`switch`,`pikachu`,`eevee`]},{id:`game-lgpe-catch`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Let's Go catching is a GO-like throw, not a grass battle first`,stat:`CATCH`,sub:`Motion or stick. Partner on your shoulder, like Yellow.`,why:`In Let's Go, wild Pokémon appear in the overworld and you throw a ball, similar to Pokémon GO. Trainer battles are still turn-based. The partner Pikachu or Eevee rides with you.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`switch`,`go`,`pikachu`,`eevee`]},{id:`game-meltan`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Meltan is a Mythical that originated in Pokémon GO`,stat:`GO`,sub:`Let's Go can make more Meltan. Melmetal is the evolution.`,why:`Meltan first appeared in Pokémon GO in 2018. Let's Go Pikachu and Let's Go Eevee can generate Meltan for GO via a special box. Melmetal evolves in GO, not in Let's Go's Kanto dex as a wild spawn.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`go`,`switch`]},{id:`game-lgpe-153`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Let's Go's Kanto Pokédex has 153 entries because of Meltan and Melmetal`,stat:`153`,sub:`Original 151 plus two Mythicals from GO.`,why:`Let's Go numbers Meltan as 152 and Melmetal as 153 on its Kanto list. The classic 151 are still there. Those two extra slots do not exist in Red and Blue.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`dex`,`switch`,`go`]},{id:`game-swsh`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sword and Shield were the first main-series games on Switch`,stat:`2019`,sub:`November 15, 2019. Galar. Dynamax. $59.99 launch MSRP.`,why:`Pokémon Sword and Shield launched November 15, 2019, on Nintendo Switch at $59.99 USD. They take place in Galar. Dynamax and Gigantamax exist in these games. They are video games, not the Sword & Shield TCG era's street dates.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-swsh-msrp`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sword and Shield launched at $59.99 USD on Switch`,stat:`$59.99`,sub:`Nintendo's launch sticker. Not a used-cart sold.`,why:`The standard Switch versions of Sword and Shield listed at $59.99 USD at launch in 2019. That is MSRP. Catch'em will not invent an eBay sold for a loose cart.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-galar-400`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Galar Pokédex has 400 entries`,stat:`400`,sub:`Grookey through Eternatus. DLC adds two more lists.`,why:`Sword and Shield's base Galar Pokédex has 400 entries, ending at Eternatus. The Isle of Armor and Crown Tundra each add their own regional lists on top. Paldea later also used a 400-entry regional dex.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`dex`,`switch`]},{id:`game-dexit`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sword and Shield did not include every National Dex species`,stat:`DEXIT`,sub:`Galar's 400. Older monsters waited on HOME or later games.`,why:`Not every existing Pokémon can be caught or transferred into Sword and Shield. Fans called that cut Dexit. Pokémon HOME later stored species that Galar would not take. The National Dex as a complete in-game list is gone from that pair.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`dex`,`switch`,`home`]},{id:`game-national-dex-removed`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sword and Shield dropped the in-game National Pokédex`,stat:`NO NAT`,sub:`Regional dex only. HOME holds the bigger box.`,why:`From Sword and Shield on, main games no longer ship a browsable National Dex of every species. You get a regional list. Pokémon HOME is the cloud box that still sees the larger census.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`dex`,`switch`,`home`]},{id:`game-dynamax`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Dynamax makes a Pokémon giant for three turns in Galar`,stat:`2019`,sub:`Gyms, stadiums, Max Raid dens. Every species can Dynamax.`,why:`Dynamax is Sword and Shield's gimmick: the Pokémon grows huge for three turns. It exists in Galar gyms, stadiums, and Max Raid dens. It does not return as a battle rule in Paldea or Z-A.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-gigantamax`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Gigantamax is a new look, and only some species can do it`,stat:`GMAX`,sub:`Rarer than Dynamax. The body actually changes. Galar only.`,why:`Gigantamax is a Dynamax variant where the Pokémon's model changes and it gains a G-Max move. Only certain species, and often a special factor, can Gigantamax. Dynamax is the generic giant form. Both stay in Galar.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-wild-area`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sword and Shield's Wild Area is a connected overworld with weather and raids`,stat:`WILD`,sub:`Red beam dens are Max Raids. Not Paldea's open map.`,why:`The Wild Area sits in the middle of Galar as a large connected field with changing weather and Max Raid dens. Paldea's open world is a later, bigger design. Do not mix the two maps.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-isle-of-armor`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Isle of Armor DLC landed June 17, 2020`,stat:`2020`,sub:`First half of the Sword and Shield Expansion Pass. Urshifu.`,why:`The Isle of Armor released June 17, 2020, as part one of the Sword and Shield Expansion Pass. It adds a dojo island, Slowpoke regional form, and Kubfu into Urshifu. It is paid DLC, not a new boxed pair.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`],asOf:`2020-06-17`},{id:`game-crown-tundra`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Crown Tundra DLC landed October 22, 2020`,stat:`2020`,sub:`Second half. Calyrex. Legendary hunts in the snow.`,why:`The Crown Tundra released October 22, 2020, as part two of the Sword and Shield Expansion Pass. Calyrex, Glastrier, Spectrier, and a legendary-hunting story sit there. Expansion Pass launch MSRP was $29.99 USD.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`],asOf:`2020-10-22`},{id:`game-gloria-swsh`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Gloria is the Galar girl. The boxes are Zacian and Zamazenta`,stat:`GLORIA`,sub:`Victor is the boy. Cover wolves are the legends.`,why:`Sword's box is Zacian and Shield's box is Zamazenta. Gloria and Victor are the trainers, and Hop is the rival.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-hm-removed`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sword and Shield removed Hidden Machines`,stat:`NO HM`,sub:`Charms and the bike do field work. Moveslots stay for battle.`,why:`Galar does not teach Cut or Surf as required HMs. Key Items and the bicycle handle exploration. Alola had already moved field tools onto Ride Pokémon. Paldea continues without classic HMs.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-galarian-forms`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Galarian forms are Galar's regional variants`,stat:`GALAR`,sub:`Weezing, Zigzagoon, Ponyta, Articuno, Zapdos, Moltres, and more.`,why:`Sword and Shield add Galarian forms with new typing, including the Galarian legendary birds in the Crown Tundra. They share National numbers with the original forms. Alolan forms came first, in 2016.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-bdsp`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Brilliant Diamond and Shining Pearl remade Diamond and Pearl in 2021`,stat:`2021`,sub:`November 19. Switch. ILCA developed them. $59.99 launch MSRP.`,why:`Pokémon Brilliant Diamond and Shining Pearl launched November 19, 2021, on Switch at $59.99 USD. ILCA developed the remakes, with Game Freak supervising. They follow Diamond and Pearl more closely than Platinum.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`switch`]},{id:`game-bdsp-ilca`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`ILCA built Brilliant Diamond and Shining Pearl, not Game Freak solo`,stat:`ILCA`,sub:`Faithful chibi remakes. Grand Underground instead of Platinum's extras.`,why:`ILCA developed BDSP. The games restore the chibi overworld and the 151-entry Sinnoh dex, not Platinum's 210. The Grand Underground replaces the old underground with a larger sandbox.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`switch`]},{id:`game-pla`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Arceus launched January 28, 2022, in Hisui`,stat:`2022`,sub:`Old Sinnoh. Open catching. $59.99 launch MSRP on Switch.`,why:`Pokémon Legends: Arceus launched January 28, 2022, on Switch at $59.99 USD. Hisui is the old name for the Sinnoh region. You catch Pokémon in the open instead of a grass battle first.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`sinnoh`,`switch`,`arceus`]},{id:`game-hisui-name`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Hisui is ancient Sinnoh, not Paldea and not modern Sinnoh-now`,stat:`HISUI`,sub:`Same land, older time. Diamond and Pearl are the modern games.`,why:`Legends Arceus is set in Hisui, the old name for Sinnoh. Jubilife is a village. Modern Diamond, Pearl, Platinum, and BDSP are the present-day region. Do not file Hisuian forms as Paldean.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`sinnoh`,`switch`]},{id:`game-hisui-242`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Hisui Pokédex has 242 entries`,stat:`242`,sub:`Arceus is the last. Smaller than Galar's 400.`,why:`Legends Arceus uses a 242-entry Hisui Pokédex. Completing pages by catching, not only seeing, is the research loop. It is a different census from the 151-entry modern Sinnoh dex.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`dex`,`switch`]},{id:`game-pla-catch`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Arceus lets you throw a ball in the open field`,stat:`CATCH`,sub:`Backstrikes. Aggro. Space-time distortions. Turn-based if you battle.`,why:`In Hisui, wild Pokémon roam. You can sneak and throw a ball without entering a battle. If you fight, battles are still turn-based. That loop is not Terastal and not Dynamax.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`switch`]},{id:`game-hisuian-forms`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Hisuian forms are the ancient regional variants from Legends Arceus`,stat:`HISUI`,sub:`Growlithe, Voltorb, Zorua, Typhlosion, Samurott, Decidueye, and more.`,why:`Hisuian forms change typing and often the evolution method, such as Hisuian Qwilfish into Overqwil. They are not Galarian or Paldean forms. National numbers stay with the original species.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`switch`]},{id:`game-pla-origin`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Arceus added Origin Forme Dialga and Palkia`,stat:`ORIGIN`,sub:`Story items. Not Platinum's Giratina Origin by itself.`,why:`In Hisui, Dialga and Palkia gain Origin Formes used in the climax. Giratina's Origin Forme had already appeared in Platinum. Same word, different Pokémon. Arceus is the title legend, not the only cover idea.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`sinnoh`,`switch`,`arceus`]},{id:`game-akari-pla`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Akari is the Hisui girl. Rei is the boy. The title legend is Arceus`,stat:`AKARI`,sub:`Galaxy Team uniforms. Cover is not the girl.`,why:`Legends Arceus lets you play Akari or Rei as a Galaxy Team surveyor. Arceus is the namesake legendary. Hisui is the setting. Box art still is not a substitute for the player character.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`switch`,`arceus`]},{id:`game-national-905`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`After Legends Arceus the National Dex was 905`,stat:`905`,sub:`Enamorus is 905. Galar's new species plus seven Hisui names.`,why:`Sword and Shield plus DLC reached 898. Legends Arceus added Wyrdeer, Kleavor, Ursaluna, Basculegion, Sneasler, Overqwil, and Enamorus, closing Generation VIII at 905.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`dex`,`galar`]},{id:`game-sv`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Scarlet and Violet opened Paldea on November 18, 2022`,stat:`2022`,sub:`Switch open world. Terastal. $59.99 launch MSRP.`,why:`Pokémon Scarlet and Violet launched November 18, 2022, on Switch at $59.99 USD. Paldea is an open map with three story paths. Terastal is the gimmick. The TCG Scarlet & Violet era is paper that followed, not the cartridge.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-sv-msrp`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Scarlet and Violet launched at $59.99 USD`,stat:`$59.99`,sub:`Switch launch sticker. Not a used sold. Not a sealed TCG box.`,why:`Nintendo listed Scarlet and Violet at $59.99 USD at launch in 2022. That is MSRP for the game. Catch'em will not invent an eBay sold or a booster-box price from that number.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-paldea-400`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Paldea Pokédex has 400 entries`,stat:`400`,sub:`Sprigatito through Miraidon or Koraidon. Same count as Galar's base dex.`,why:`Scarlet and Violet's Paldea Pokédex has 400 entries. Kitakami and Blueberry Academy add separate DLC dexes. Four hundred is a regional count, not the National Dex.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`dex`,`switch`]},{id:`game-paldea-120`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Paldea added 120 new Pokémon through the DLC`,stat:`120`,sub:`Sprigatito through Pecharunt. National Dex hit 1025.`,why:`Generation IX introduced 120 new National Dex species, numbers 906 through 1025. The base games plus The Teal Mask and The Indigo Disk, including Pecharunt, close that count.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`dex`]},{id:`game-national-1025`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`After Scarlet and Violet's DLC the National Dex was 1025`,stat:`1025`,sub:`Pecharunt is 1025. Winds and Waves will open Generation X.`,why:`The National Dex stood at 1025 after Paldea's DLC mythicals. That is a game census. It is not a TCG set number and not a reason to mix card street dates with cartridge launches.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`dex`]},{id:`game-terastal`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Terastal is Scarlet and Violet's crystal type change`,stat:`TERA`,sub:`A gem on the head. Type can change. They do not go giant.`,why:`Terastallizing puts a crystal on the Pokémon and can change its type for that battle. It is Paldea's gimmick. Dynamax was the giant form in Galar only. Mega Evolution is a different transformation.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-open-world-sv`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Scarlet and Violet are the first fully open-world main-series pair`,stat:`OPEN`,sub:`Three paths: Victory Road, Starfall Street, Path of Legends.`,why:`Paldea lets you take gyms, Team Star, and Titan Pokémon in any order from an open map. Sword and Shield still funneled you along a route spine with a Wild Area in the middle. Different structures.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-sv-academy`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Scarlet and Violet are school games: Naranja or Uva Academy`,stat:`SCHOOL`,sub:`Scarlet is Naranja. Violet is Uva. Nemona is the rival.`,why:`You enroll at Naranja Academy in Scarlet or Uva Academy in Violet. The Treasure Hunt is the year's project. That school frame is Paldea-only. Kitakami is a later trip.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-picnic`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Picnics and sandwiches are Scarlet and Violet's overworld cookout`,stat:`PICNIC`,sub:`Eggs. Sparkling Power. Not a pack trick.`,why:`In Paldea you picnic to make eggs and eat sandwiches that boost encounter types or shiny odds in the overworld. The Masuda method is still two-language parents. Sandwiches do not change pack odds, because this is not the TCG.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`,`shiny`]},{id:`game-juliana-sv`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Juliana is the Paldea girl. The boxes are Koraidon and Miraidon`,stat:`JULIANA`,sub:`Florian is the boy. Cover legends are the ride paradoxes.`,why:`Scarlet's box is Koraidon and Violet's box is Miraidon. Juliana and Florian are the trainers, and those box legends are also your ride Pokémon. Cover and player are still not the same person.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-teal-mask`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Teal Mask DLC launched September 13, 2023, in Kitakami`,stat:`2023`,sub:`Part one of The Hidden Treasure of Area Zero. Ogerpon.`,why:`The Teal Mask released September 13, 2023. You travel to Kitakami, meet Ogerpon and the Loyal Three, and fill a separate Kitakami Pokédex. It is paid Scarlet and Violet DLC, not a new boxed generation.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`],asOf:`2023-09-13`},{id:`game-kitakami`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Kitakami Pokédex has 200 entries`,stat:`200`,sub:`DLC regional list. Not Paldea's 400.`,why:`The Teal Mask uses a 200-entry Kitakami Pokédex. Kitakami is a land of folklore east of Paldea, not a Paldea province. Completing it is separate from the Paldea dex.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`dex`,`switch`]},{id:`game-indigo-disk`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Indigo Disk DLC launched December 14, 2023, at Blueberry Academy`,stat:`2023`,sub:`Part two. Terarium. Terapagos. Expansion Pass was $34.99 USD.`,why:`The Indigo Disk released December 14, 2023. Blueberry Academy sits under the sea with a four-biome Terarium. Terapagos is the new legendary. The Hidden Treasure of Area Zero Expansion Pass launched at $34.99 USD.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`],asOf:`2023-12-14`},{id:`game-blueberry`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Blueberry Pokédex has 243 entries`,stat:`243`,sub:`Terarium list. Unova school, not a Unova remake.`,why:`Blueberry Academy, associated with Unova, uses a 243-entry Blueberry Pokédex. It is DLC inside Scarlet and Violet, not Black and White remade. Returning species live in the Terarium biomes.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`unova`,`dex`,`switch`]},{id:`game-paradox`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Paradox Pokémon are ancient or future relatives, not regional forms`,stat:`PARADOX`,sub:`Great Tusk versus Iron Treads. Scarlet past, Violet future.`,why:`Scarlet's past Paradox Pokémon and Violet's future Paradox Pokémon are version-exclusive Area Zero creatures. They are new species with new dex numbers, not Paldean forms of existing ones. Koraidon and Miraidon sit in that family.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-paldean-forms`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Paldean forms include Wooper and three Tauros breeds`,stat:`PALDEA`,sub:`Paldean Wooper is Poison/Ground and evolves into Clodsire.`,why:`Scarlet and Violet add Paldean regional forms. Paldean Wooper is Poison/Ground and evolves into Clodsire, a new species. Paldean Tauros has Combat, Blaze, and Aqua breeds. Regional form is not the same as Paradox.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-convergent`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Wiglett and Toedscool are convergent species, not regional Diglett and Tentacool`,stat:`FAKE`,sub:`New dex numbers. They only look related.`,why:`Convergent Pokémon resemble older species but have their own National Dex numbers. Wiglett is not Paldean Diglett. Toedscool is not Paldean Tentacool. Regional forms keep the old number.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`dex`,`switch`]},{id:`game-tera-raid`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Tera Raid Battles are Paldea's seven-star co-op crystal raids`,stat:`RAID`,sub:`1- to 7-star. Online or local. Not Max Raids.`,why:`Tera Raids are Scarlet and Violet's co-op battles against a Terastallized wild Pokémon. Max Raids were Galar's Dynamax dens. Same idea of a group hunt, different gimmick and games.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`,`play`]},{id:`game-za`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Z-A launched October 16, 2025, in Lumiose City`,stat:`2025`,sub:`Kalos, five years after X and Y. Mega Evolution is back.`,why:`Pokémon Legends: Z-A launched October 16, 2025, on Nintendo Switch and as a Nintendo Switch 2 Edition. It is set in Lumiose City. Real-time battles and Mega Evolution are the headline systems. It is a video game date, not a TCG Mega Evolution street date.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`,`switch2`,`mega`],asOf:`2025-10-16`},{id:`game-za-msrp`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Z-A launched at $59.99 on Switch and $69.99 on Switch 2`,stat:`$59.99`,sub:`Switch 2 Edition is $69.99. A $9.99 upgrade pack exists.`,why:`Nintendo listed Pokémon Legends: Z-A at $59.99 USD for Nintendo Switch and $69.99 USD for the Nintendo Switch 2 Edition on October 16, 2025. A $9.99 Upgrade Pack moves the Switch version to Switch 2. Those are launch stickers, not used solds.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`,`switch2`],asOf:`2025-10-16`},{id:`game-za-lumiose`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Z-A takes place entirely in Lumiose City`,stat:`LUMIOSE`,sub:`Kalos capital. Quasartico redevelopment. Not a full-region Kalos remake.`,why:`Z-A is set in Lumiose City five years after X and Y, with Quasartico running an urban redevelopment plan. You do not walk all of Kalos the way X and Y did. Hotel Z and Team MZ are your base.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`,`switch2`]},{id:`game-za-five-years`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Z-A is five years after the plot of X and Y`,stat:`5 YR`,sub:`Same city, later time. AZ is at Hotel Z.`,why:`The game states it is five years after X and Y. AZ, the thousand-year king from those games, owns Hotel Z. Team Flare's leftover weapon still matters. It is a sequel-in-place, not a prequel like Hisui.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`]},{id:`game-za-realtime`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Z-A battles play out in real time, not waiting on a menu first`,stat:`REAL`,sub:`Move cooldowns. Mega still hits in that fight.`,why:`Z-A uses real-time battles: you dodge and fire moves on cooldown instead of picking from a pause menu the way Scarlet and Violet do. Mega Evolution is woven into that combat. It is still a main-series Pokémon game.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`,`switch2`,`mega`]},{id:`game-za-mega`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Z-A brought Mega Evolution back as a main-series gimmick in 2025`,stat:`MEGA`,sub:`First time since the 3DS. New Megas include starters from older gens.`,why:`Mega Evolution left the main series after the 3DS games. Z-A restored it in 2025 in Lumiose, including new Mega forms such as Mega Meganium, Mega Feraligatr, and Mega Emboar. The 2025 TCG Mega Evolution block is paper inspired by that revival.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`,`switch2`,`mega`]},{id:`game-lumiose-232`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Lumiose Pokédex has 232 entries`,stat:`232`,sub:`Z-A's city list. Hyperspace is a separate DLC dex.`,why:`Legends Z-A uses a 232-entry Lumiose Pokédex. The Mega Dimension DLC adds a Hyperspace Pokédex with 132 more slots for species that are not in the city. Two lists, one game family.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`dex`,`switch`]},{id:`game-mega-dimension`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Mega Dimension, the Z-A DLC, launched December 10, 2025`,stat:`DLC`,sub:`Hyperspace Lumiose. Hoopa. Rogue Megas.`,why:`Pokémon Legends: Z-A's paid DLC Mega Dimension released December 10, 2025. Hoopa opens Hyperspace Lumiose, where Pokémon can go Rogue Mega. It is DLC for Z-A, not a new numbered generation.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`,`switch2`,`mega`],asOf:`2025-12-10`},{id:`game-taunie-za`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Taunie and Urbain lead Team MZ in Legends Z-A`,stat:`TEAM MZ`,sub:`Play as a new arrival. They meet you at Hotel Z.`,why:`In Legends Z-A you arrive in Lumiose and join Team MZ. If you present as a boy, Taunie is your partner. If you present as a girl, Urbain is.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`]},{id:`game-champions`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Champions launched April 8, 2026, on Switch and Switch 2`,stat:`APR 8`,sub:`Ranked battles. Mega from Z-A. Not the TCG World Championships.`,why:`Pokémon Champions launched April 8, 2026, on Nintendo Switch and Nintendo Switch 2. The Pokémon Works developed it. It is a battle game that became the VGC platform. It is not the trading-card Worlds bracket.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch`,`switch2`,`play`],asOf:`2026-04-08`},{id:`game-champions-f2p`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Champions is free to play on Switch, with a paid Starter Pack`,stat:`F2P`,sub:`Mobile followed later in 2026. Cross-play with phones is planned.`,why:`Pokémon Champions launched as a free download on Switch on April 8, 2026. A paid Starter Pack adds storage and extras. A mobile version arrived later in 2026. Launch MSRP for the base game is $0.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch`,`switch2`,`phone`,`play`],asOf:`2026-04-08`},{id:`game-champions-mega`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Champions battles include Mega-Evolved Pokémon from Legends Z-A`,stat:`MEGA`,sub:`Mega Meganium, Mega Emboar, Mega Feraligatr can appear here too.`,why:`Pokémon Champions uses main-series battle rules and carries forward Mega Evolution, including Megas introduced in Z-A. It links with Pokémon HOME. Skins and ranks here do not set TCG solds.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch`,`play`,`mega`]},{id:`game-winds-waves`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Winds and Waves are the Generation X pair, announced for Switch 2 in 2027`,stat:`2027`,sub:`Announced Pokémon Day 2026. Not out in 2026.`,why:`Pokémon Winds and Pokémon Waves were announced on February 27, 2026, for Nintendo Switch 2 in 2027. They are the Generation X main-series pair. A 2027 window is not a street date, and it is not a TCG launch.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch2`],asOf:`2026-02-27`},{id:`game-switch2-2025`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Nintendo Switch 2 launched June 5, 2025`,stat:`6/5`,sub:`Z-A, Pokopia, Champions, and Winds and Waves are the Pokémon slate.`,why:`Nintendo Switch 2 launched worldwide on June 5, 2025, at $449.99 USD for the console. It plays Switch Pokémon games. New Pokémon software on it includes Legends Z-A's Switch 2 Edition, Pokopia, Champions, and later Winds and Waves.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch2`],asOf:`2025-06-05`},{id:`game-stadium`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Stadium put Kanto battles in 3D on Nintendo 64`,stat:`N64`,sub:`Japan 1999, North America 2000. Transfer from Game Boy.`,why:`Pokémon Stadium on Nintendo 64 let you battle the 151 Kanto species in 3D and upload parties from Red, Blue, and Yellow. Japan's earlier Pocket Monsters Stadium only had a subset. The NA game is the full-roster Stadium.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`n64`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-stadium2`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Stadium 2 added Johto and mixed Gen 1–2 battles on N64`,stat:`N64`,sub:`Japan 2000, North America 2001. Gym Leader Castle returns.`,why:`Pokémon Stadium 2 launched in Japan on December 14, 2000, and in North America on March 26, 2001. It supports Gold, Silver, and Crystal transfers and includes Johto. It is a battle stadium, not a new RPG region.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`n64`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-colosseum`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Colosseum is the 2003 GameCube RPG set in Orre`,stat:`GC`,sub:`Japan November 21, 2003. North America March 22, 2004.`,why:`Pokémon Colosseum, by Genius Sonority, launched in Japan on November 21, 2003, and in North America on March 22, 2004. You play Wes in the Orre region and snag Shadow Pokémon from Team Cipher. It links to Ruby and Sapphire.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`orre`,`gc`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-shadow`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Shadow Pokémon have closed hearts until you purify them`,stat:`SHADOW`,sub:`Colosseum and XD. Snag, then purify. Not a Dark-type label.`,why:`In Colosseum and XD, Cipher turns Pokémon into Shadow Pokémon. You snag them in battle, then purify them so they can gain happiness and stronger moves. Shadow is a story status, not the Dark type.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`orre`,`gc`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-xd`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`XD: Gale of Darkness is the 2005 Orre sequel with Shadow Lugia`,stat:`XD`,sub:`Japan August 4, 2005. North America October 3, 2005. GameCube.`,why:`Pokémon XD: Gale of Darkness launched in 2005 on GameCube. You play Michael in Orre and face Shadow Lugia, nicknamed XD001. It is a sequel to Colosseum, not a main-series Hoenn game.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`orre`,`gc`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-ranger`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Ranger is a 2006 DS game where you capture with a styler`,stat:`DS`,sub:`Loop the touch screen. You do not train a party of six to level 100.`,why:`Pokémon Ranger launched in Japan on March 23, 2006, and in North America on October 30, 2006. You draw circles around wild Pokémon with the Capture Styler. When the mission ends, they leave. It is not a main-series RPG.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`ds`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-ranger-manaphy`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The first Ranger could send a Manaphy Egg into Diamond and Pearl`,stat:`EGG`,sub:`One-time DS transfer. That is how many players got Manaphy.`,why:`Pokémon Ranger includes a mission that produces a Manaphy Egg you can transfer to Diamond or Pearl. It was a real in-game distribution, not a store mystery gift. Later Ranger games had their own extra missions.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`ds`,`sinnoh`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-ranger-almia`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ranger: Shadows of Almia is the 2008 second Ranger game`,stat:`2008`,sub:`Almia region. Still a styler, still DS.`,why:`Pokémon Ranger: Shadows of Almia launched in Japan on March 20, 2008, and in North America on November 10, 2008. A new region, Almia, and a new Ranger School story. Capture is still the styler.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`ds`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-ranger-guardian`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ranger: Guardian Signs is the 2010 third Ranger game`,stat:`2010`,sub:`Oblivia. Last main Ranger title on DS.`,why:`Pokémon Ranger: Guardian Signs launched in Japan on March 6, 2010, and in North America on October 4, 2010. It is set in Oblivia and is the last numbered Ranger game. There was no Switch Ranger by 2026.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`ds`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-md-rescue`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Mystery Dungeon Rescue Team put you in a dungeon as a Pokémon`,stat:`2005`,sub:`Red on GBA, Blue on DS. You are the monster.`,why:`Pokémon Mystery Dungeon: Red Rescue Team and Blue Rescue Team launched in Japan on November 17, 2005. You wake up as a Pokémon and recruit a team through dungeons. Spike Chunsoft (then Chunsoft) made them, not Game Freak's main RPG team.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`gba`,`ds`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-md-you-are`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`In Mystery Dungeon you are a Pokémon, not a human trainer`,stat:`YOU`,sub:`Personality quiz picks the species. Recruits join the team.`,why:`Mystery Dungeon games start with a quiz that assigns you a species. You explore floor-based dungeons, not gyms. Recruited Pokémon join the rescue team. It is a spin-off loop, not the main series.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-md-explorers`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Explorers of Time and Darkness moved Mystery Dungeon to a new continent in 2007`,stat:`2007`,sub:`DS. Explorers of Sky in 2009 is the complete version.`,why:`Pokémon Mystery Dungeon: Explorers of Time and Explorers of Darkness launched in Japan on September 13, 2007. Explorers of Sky, 2009, is the third-version-style complete game. Wigglytuff's Guild is the hub.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`ds`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-md-sky`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Explorers of Sky is the definitive Explorers Mystery Dungeon`,stat:`2009`,sub:`Extra dungeons and episodes on top of Time and Darkness.`,why:`Pokémon Mystery Dungeon: Explorers of Sky launched in Japan on April 18, 2009, and in North America on October 12, 2009. It includes Time and Darkness content plus new episodes. Many fans treat Sky as the one to play.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`ds`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-md-gates`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Gates to Infinity took Mystery Dungeon to 3DS in 2012`,stat:`3DS`,sub:`First 3D-looking MD. Smaller roster than Explorers.`,why:`Pokémon Mystery Dungeon: Gates to Infinity launched in Japan on November 23, 2012, and in North America on March 24, 2013, on 3DS. It is a new MD story with a reduced recruit list compared with Explorers of Sky.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`3ds`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-md-super`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Super Mystery Dungeon is the 2015 3DS crossover of many species`,stat:`2015`,sub:`Japan September 17. West November 20. Huge recruit list.`,why:`Pokémon Super Mystery Dungeon launched in 2015 on 3DS. It lets you recruit a very large share of the National Dex for a Mystery Dungeon game. It is still a dungeon crawler, not a gym quest.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`3ds`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-md-dx`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Rescue Team DX remade the first Mystery Dungeon on Switch in 2020`,stat:`2020`,sub:`March 6. Watercolor look. Same premise: you are a Pokémon.`,why:`Pokémon Mystery Dungeon: Rescue Team DX launched March 6, 2020, on Switch. It remakes Red and Blue Rescue Team with new visuals. You are still a Pokémon running a rescue team, not a Paldea student.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-conquest`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Conquest is a 2012 DS strategy game set in Ransei`,stat:`2012`,sub:`Samurai kingdoms. One Pokémon per warrior. Not a gym quest.`,why:`Pokémon Conquest launched in Japan on March 17, 2012, and in North America on June 18, 2012. Intelligent Systems mixed Pokémon with a Nobunaga-style war map. Each warrior links to one Pokémon. It is a spin-off.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`ds`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-snap`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Snap is a 1999 N64 rail shooter where the gun is a camera`,stat:`N64`,sub:`Japan March 21, 1999. North America July 5, 1999. Professor Oak grades shots.`,why:`Pokémon Snap, by HAL Laboratory, launched on Nintendo 64 in 1999. You ride a vehicle through courses and photograph Pokémon. Oak scores the album. It is not a battle RPG.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`n64`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-new-snap`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`New Pokémon Snap brought the camera to Switch on April 30, 2021`,stat:`2021`,sub:`Lental region. More courses, night shots, and later DLC.`,why:`New Pokémon Snap launched April 30, 2021, on Switch. You photograph Pokémon in the Lental region. It is a sequel to the 1999 N64 game, not a main-series map.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-go-2016`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon GO launched in the United States on July 6, 2016`,stat:`2016`,sub:`Phones. Niantic. Map walking, not a gym quest on a cartridge.`,why:`Pokémon GO launched on iOS and Android in 2016, reaching the US on July 6. Niantic built it. You walk real streets, throw balls, and raid. It is a live phone game, not Sword and Shield.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`go`,`phone`]},{id:`game-go-scopely`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Scopely bought Niantic's games, including GO, for $3.5 billion`,stat:`$3.5B`,sub:`Closed May 29, 2025. That is a studio deal, not a TCG sold.`,why:`Scopely closed the purchase of Niantic's games business on May 29, 2025, for $3.5 billion. Pokémon GO, Pikmin Bloom, and Monster Hunter Now moved with that deal. The figure is a corporate price, not a card sale.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`go`,`phone`],asOf:`2025-05-29`},{id:`game-unite`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`UNITE is a 5v5 MOBA, not the official VGC circuit`,stat:`MOBA`,sub:`Switch July 21, 2021. Phones September 22, 2021. TiMi / Tencent.`,why:`Pokémon UNITE launched on Nintendo Switch on July 21, 2021, and on mobile on September 22, 2021. It is a free-to-start team battler by TiMi Studio Group. Ranked UNITE is not VGC, and skins do not move paper prices.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unite`,`switch`,`phone`,`play`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-sleep`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Sleep launched July 17, 2023, and tracks real sleep`,stat:`SLEEP`,sub:`Phones. Research while you rest. Not a main-series cartridge.`,why:`Pokémon Sleep launched July 17, 2023, on phones. It uses sleep tracking to hunt hungry Pokémon at camp. Select Button and The Pokémon Company made it. It is not Scarlet and Violet's picnic.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`phone`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-cafe`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Café Mix is a 2020 phone puzzle game, later Café ReMix`,stat:`CAFE`,sub:`Match puzzles in a café. Switch version arrived with ReMix.`,why:`Pokémon Café Mix launched June 24, 2020, on phones as a café puzzle game. It was rebuilt as Pokémon Café ReMix, which also came to Switch. Staffing a café is not a gym badge.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`phone`,`switch`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-pokopia`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokopia launched March 5, 2026, as a Switch 2 life sim`,stat:`3/5`,sub:`Cozy building. Koei Tecmo's Omega Force with Game Freak.`,why:`Pokémon Pokopia launched worldwide on March 5, 2026, exclusively on Nintendo Switch 2. Omega Force and Game Freak made a life-sim where you shape land with Pokémon moves. It is a real spin-off, not a rumor.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch2`,`spinoff`],asOf:`2026-03-05`},{id:`game-pokopia-ditto`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`In Pokopia you play as a Ditto that has taken a human shape`,stat:`DITTO`,sub:`Learn moves from friends. Grow grass with Bulbasaur. Water with Squirtle.`,why:`Pokopia's player is a Ditto pretending to be human. You befriend Pokémon and copy their moves to reshape the island, then build homes. It is not a battle-gym quest and not Animal Crossing with a Pikachu hat only.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch2`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-pokopia-msrp`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokopia launched at $69.99 USD on Switch 2`,stat:`$69.99`,sub:`Switch 2 exclusive. Launch sticker, not a used sold.`,why:`Nintendo listed Pokémon Pokopia at $69.99 USD at launch on March 5, 2026. That is MSRP for the Switch 2 exclusive. Catch'em will not invent an eBay sold for a used copy.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch2`,`spinoff`],asOf:`2026-03-05`},{id:`game-home`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon HOME launched in February 2020 as cloud storage`,stat:`HOME`,sub:`Switch plus phones. The box that outlived the National Dex in-game.`,why:`Pokémon HOME launched February 12, 2020, on Nintendo Switch and mobile. It stores Pokémon across games and replaced Pokémon Bank as the long-term box. Galar's missing species live here even when a cartridge will not take them.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`home`,`switch`,`phone`]},{id:`game-bank`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Bank was the 3DS cloud box before HOME`,stat:`BANK`,sub:`Japan 2013, West 2014. Moves 3DS games toward HOME.`,why:`Pokémon Bank launched on 3DS in late 2013 in Japan and in 2014 in the West. Combined with Poké Transporter, it pulled Pokémon out of older DS games. HOME is the Switch-era successor.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`3ds`,`home`]},{id:`game-bank-end-2027`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Bank is scheduled to end service on February 25, 2027`,stat:`2027`,sub:`Move 3DS boxes into HOME before that date.`,why:`Nintendo set Pokémon Bank's end of service for February 25, 2027, at 7:00 p.m. PT. Pokémon still in Bank need to go to HOME before shutdown. That is a server date, not a TCG print stop.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`3ds`,`home`],asOf:`2027-02-25`},{id:`game-pokken`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokkén Tournament is a fighting game, first on Wii U, then DX on Switch`,stat:`FIGHT`,sub:`Bandai Namco. DX launched September 22, 2017, on Switch.`,why:`Pokkén Tournament launched on Wii U in 2016 in the West after a 2015 Japan arcade and Wii U run. Pokkén Tournament DX launched on Switch on September 22, 2017. It is a fighting game, not VGC.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch`,`wiiu`,`spinoff`,`play`]},{id:`game-battle-revolution`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Battle Revolution was the Wii stadium game that took DS teams`,stat:`WII`,sub:`Japan 2006, North America June 25, 2007. Colosseums, not a new RPG.`,why:`Pokémon Battle Revolution launched on Wii in 2006 in Japan and on June 25, 2007, in North America. You upload Diamond and Pearl teams and battle in 3D colosseums. It is a stadium spin-off, like Stadium on N64.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`wii`,`sinnoh`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-masters`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Masters EX is a 2019 phone game of sync pairs`,stat:`2019`,sub:`Trainers plus Pokémon as pairs. Not VGC on a Switch.`,why:`Pokémon Masters launched August 29, 2019, on phones, later retitled Masters EX. You battle with trainer-and-Pokémon sync pairs. It is a gacha-style phone game, not the official video-game championships.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`phone`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-detective`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Detective Pikachu is a 3DS mystery game. Returns came to Switch in 2023`,stat:`PIKA`,sub:`Japan 2016, West 2018. Returns: October 6, 2023.`,why:`Detective Pikachu launched on 3DS in Japan in 2016 and in the West on March 23, 2018. Detective Pikachu Returns launched October 6, 2023, on Switch. You solve cases with a talking Pikachu. It is not the live-action movie by itself.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`3ds`,`switch`,`pikachu`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-friends`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Friends launched July 22, 2025, as a puzzle game`,stat:`2025`,sub:`Phones and Switch. Wonderfy. Not a main-series pair.`,why:`Pokémon Friends launched July 22, 2025, on iOS, Android, and Nintendo Switch, announced the same day. Wonderfy and h.a.n.d. made an education-leaning puzzle game. It is a spin-off, not Winds and Waves.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`phone`,`switch`,`spinoff`],asOf:`2025-07-22`},{id:`game-smile`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Smile is a 2020 phone game that watches you brush your teeth`,stat:`SMILE`,sub:`Camera. Kids. Not a gym, not GO.`,why:`Pokémon Smile launched in 2020 on phones. It uses the camera to check toothbrushing and then plays a short catch-and-care loop. It is a real official game, and it is not the main series.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`phone`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-ranch`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`My Pokémon Ranch stored Diamond and Pearl teams on WiiWare in 2008`,stat:`RANCH`,sub:`Wii. Hayley. A box in a meadow, not a new region.`,why:`My Pokémon Ranch launched on WiiWare in 2008. You deposit Pokémon from Diamond and Pearl and watch them in a 3D ranch. It is a storage toy, later succeeded in spirit by Bank and HOME.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`wii`,`sinnoh`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-box-rs`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Box Ruby & Sapphire was a GameCube storage disc`,stat:`BOX`,sub:`2003–2004. Organize Hoenn teams on a bigger screen.`,why:`Pokémon Box Ruby & Sapphire launched on GameCube in 2003 in Japan and in 2004 in the West. It stores Ruby and Sapphire Pokémon and includes a limited Emerald-compatible emulator. HOME is the modern version of this idea.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`gc`,`hoenn`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-shiny-8192`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`From Gold through Black 2, a random wild shiny was 1 in 8,192`,stat:`1/8192`,sub:`Generations II to V. X and Y halved that to 1 in 4,096.`,why:`Generation II introduced shiny Pokémon at 1 in 8,192. That rate held through Generation V. It is a game encounter rate. It is not a TCG pull rate and not a pack-odds metaphor.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`shiny`,`gbc`,`ds`]},{id:`game-shiny-4096`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`From X and Y onward, a random wild shiny is 1 in 4,096`,stat:`1/4096`,sub:`Masuda, charms, and Paldea sandwiches change that. Not card packs.`,why:`Pokémon X and Y changed the base shiny rate to 1 in 4,096. The Masuda method, the Shiny Charm, and Scarlet and Violet sandwiches stack on top. Those are game odds. They do not describe Evolving Skies packs.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`shiny`,`kalos`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-masuda`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Masuda method is two parents from different-language games`,stat:`EGG`,sub:`Named for Junichi Masuda. Eggs only. Wild shiny is still the base rate.`,why:`Breed two Pokémon from games set to different languages and shiny eggs become more likely. That is the Masuda method, introduced in Diamond and Pearl. A random wild encounter stays at the generation's base rate unless other boosts apply.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`shiny`,`sinnoh`]},{id:`game-iv-ev`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`IVs are hidden individual values. EVs are trained effort values`,stat:`STATS`,sub:`IVs are born. EVs are earned. Natures tilt the result.`,why:`Each Pokémon has IVs set when it is generated and EVs earned from battles or items. Together with level, nature, and base stats they build the final numbers. The TCG does not use IVs.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`game-pokerus`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokérus is a rare in-game virus that doubles EV gain`,stat:`VIRUS`,sub:`Spreads in the party. Harmless. Not a real illness.`,why:`Pokérus can infect a party Pokémon and double effort-value gains while it is active. It spreads to adjacent party slots. It has existed since Gold and Silver. It is not a TCG status.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`play`,`johto`]},{id:`game-trade-evo`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Some Pokémon evolve only when traded`,stat:`TRADE`,sub:`Kadabra, Machoke, Graveler, Haunter. Items on the trade for others.`,why:`Kadabra, Machoke, Graveler, and Haunter evolve by trade in the original games. Later species need a held item during the trade, such as Seadra with a Dragon Scale. A single-player cart cannot finish those lines without help or a later workaround.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`]},{id:`game-stone-evo`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Evolutionary stones are one-time items, not friendship`,stat:`STONE`,sub:`Thunder, Fire, Water, Leaf, Moon, Sun, and later stones.`,why:`Pikachu becomes Raichu with a Thunder Stone. Eevee's first three evolutions are Water, Thunder, and Fire Stones. Espeon and Umbreon are the friendship pair instead. Moon Stone is for Clefairy and company, not Umbreon.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`eevee`]},{id:`game-friendship-evo`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Friendship evolutions include Crobat, Blissey, and Lucario`,stat:`HAPPY`,sub:`Walk and battle. Some also need day or night.`,why:`High friendship evolves Golbat into Crobat, Chansey into Blissey, Riolu into Lucario (day), and several babies into their next stage. It is a Gen 2 system that later games kept. Stones are a different door.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`]},{id:`game-regional-forms`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Regional forms keep the National number and change the typing`,stat:`FORM`,sub:`Alola first in 2016, then Galar, Hisui, Paldea.`,why:`A regional form is the same Pokédex number adapted to a new land. Alolan forms came first in Sun and Moon. Galarian, Hisuian, and Paldean forms followed. Paradox and convergent species are not regional forms.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`galar`,`hisui`,`paldea`]},{id:`game-gb-platform`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Red, Green, Blue, and Yellow shipped on Game Boy`,stat:`GB`,sub:`Monochrome original. The Western pair is Red and Blue.`,why:`The first generation is a Game Boy series. Red and Green launched in Japan in 1996. The West received Red and Blue in 1998, then Yellow. Game Boy Color games come next.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`gb`,`kanto`]},{id:`game-gbc-platform`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Gold, Silver, and Crystal are Game Boy Color games`,stat:`GBC`,sub:`Color sprites. Backward compatible with Game Boy hardware.`,why:`Generation II ran on Game Boy Color. Gold and Silver launched in Japan in 1999. Crystal in 2000 added the girl trainer and more color-only features. They still play on a original Game Boy, without color.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`gbc`,`johto`]},{id:`game-gba-platform`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ruby, Sapphire, Emerald, FireRed, and LeafGreen are Game Boy Advance games`,stat:`GBA`,sub:`Hoenn plus the Kanto remakes. Link cable, then wireless adapter.`,why:`Generation III moved to Game Boy Advance: Ruby and Sapphire opened Hoenn, FireRed and LeafGreen remade Kanto, and Emerald is the third Hoenn version. Colosseum on GameCube talks to these carts.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`gba`,`hoenn`,`kanto`]},{id:`game-gc-platform`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Colosseum, XD, and Pokémon Box ran on GameCube`,stat:`GC`,sub:`Orre RPGs plus a Hoenn storage disc. Not main-series gyms.`,why:`GameCube held Pokémon Colosseum, Pokémon XD: Gale of Darkness, and Pokémon Box Ruby & Sapphire. You can connect a GBA in the side of the cube. There is no Hoenn main RPG native to GameCube.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`gc`,`orre`]},{id:`game-n64-platform`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Stadium, Stadium 2, and Snap are the Nintendo 64 Pokémon games`,stat:`N64`,sub:`3D battles and a camera. Transfer from Game Boy carts.`,why:`Nintendo 64 never received a main-series RPG. Pokémon Stadium, Stadium 2, and Pokémon Snap are the big three. You transfer parties from Game Boy for Stadium. Snap is photographs, not gyms.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`n64`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-ds-platform`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Diamond through Black 2, plus Ranger and Conquest, ran on Nintendo DS`,stat:`DS`,sub:`Dual screen. Sinnoh, Johto remakes, Unova, and several spin-offs.`,why:`Nintendo DS hosted Diamond, Pearl, Platinum, HeartGold, SoulSilver, Black, White, Black 2, and White 2. Ranger, Mystery Dungeon Explorers, and Conquest also lived there. 3DS comes next.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`ds`,`sinnoh`,`unova`]},{id:`game-3ds-platform`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`X, Y, ORAS, Sun, Moon, and Ultra Sun and Ultra Moon are 3DS games`,stat:`3DS`,sub:`Kalos, Hoenn remakes, Alola. Last main-series handheld before Switch.`,why:`Nintendo 3DS hosted Generation VI and VII's main games, plus Mystery Dungeon Gates and Super, and Detective Pikachu. Bank lived here. Let's Go moved the main series to Switch in 2018.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`3ds`,`kalos`,`alola`]},{id:`game-switch-platform`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Switch is the home of Let's Go, Sword and Shield, BDSP, Arceus, and Scarlet and Violet`,stat:`SWITCH`,sub:`Hybrid handheld. First main-series home console pair was Sword and Shield.`,why:`Nintendo Switch launched in 2017. Let's Go arrived in 2018. Sword and Shield in 2019 were the first new-region main pair on the system. Scarlet and Violet, Legends Arceus, and the 2026 FireRed ports also run here.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch`]},{id:`game-phone-platform`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`GO, UNITE mobile, Sleep, Café, Masters, and HOME mobile are phone games`,stat:`PHONE`,sub:`iOS and Android. Live services, not Game Boy carts.`,why:`Pokémon on phones includes GO, UNITE, Sleep, Café Mix/ReMix, Masters EX, Smile, Friends, and the HOME app. They are official. They are not replacements for the boxed main series, and their events are not TCG street dates.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`phone`,`go`]},{id:`game-link-cable`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Game Boy link cable was how Gen 1 and 2 traded`,stat:`CABLE`,sub:`Two systems, one cable. Time Capsule used it too.`,why:`Red, Blue, Yellow, Gold, Silver, and Crystal trade through a physical link cable. Without a second player, trade evolutions waited. Wireless adapters and online trades come generations later.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`gb`,`gbc`]},{id:`game-nso-classics`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Nintendo Switch Online added several old Pokémon games as Classics`,stat:`NSO`,sub:`Stadium, Snap, XD, Mystery Dungeon Rescue Team. FireRed 2026 is not this.`,why:`Nintendo Switch Online + Expansion Pack has carried Pokémon Stadium, Stadium 2, Snap, Puzzle League, the GB Trading Card Game, XD: Gale of Darkness, and Rescue Team. The 2026 FireRed and LeafGreen ports are separate paid eShop games.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch`,`n64`,`gc`]},{id:`game-box-not-girl`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Crystal's English box shows Suicune`,stat:`BOX`,sub:`Suicune is on the front. Kris is the trainer named on the back.`,why:`English Pokémon Crystal puts Suicune on the box art. Kris is the trainer you can play, named on the back of the box. Cover art and playable trainer are two different things.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`crystal`,`johto`]},{id:`game-do-not-mix-tcg`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`A TCG set date is not a video-game launch`,stat:`2 CAL`,sub:`Sword the game is 2019. Sword & Shield paper is a later era.`,why:`Pokémon video games and Pokémon TCG expansions share names and art, but they do not share street dates. Scarlet and Violet the games launched November 18, 2022, while TCG Scarlet & Violet base launched in January 2023.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`galar`]},{id:`game-mythical`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Mythical Pokémon are event-distributed. Legendaries are usually in the story`,stat:`MYTH`,sub:`Mew, Celebi, Jirachi, Manaphy. You do not just trip over them.`,why:`Game Freak treats Mythical Pokémon as special event species, historically handed out with items or distributions. Cover legendaries like Groudon or Zacian are story bosses you can catch in-game. Mew is Mythical. Mewtwo is legendary.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`mew`]},{id:`game-legendary-box`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Version mascots are the box legendaries you pick a game for`,stat:`MASCOT`,sub:`Groudon or Kyogre. Zacian or Zamazenta. Koraidon or Miraidon.`,why:`Paired versions put different legendaries on the box and usually in the story. Ruby's Groudon versus Sapphire's Kyogre is the old pattern. You still play a human trainer. The girl option is not the mascot.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`]},{id:`game-starters-gfw`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Every main-series pair starts you with Grass, Fire, or Water`,stat:`G/F/W`,sub:`Bulbasaur, Charmander, Squirtle set the rule in 1996.`,why:`From Red and Green onward, the first-partner trio is Grass, Fire, and Water. Pikachu in Yellow and Let's Go is the famous exception path. Pokopia's Ditto is a spin-off, not a main-series starter.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`]},{id:`game-eevee-eight`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Eevee has eight evolutions. Sylveon closed the set in X and Y`,stat:`8`,sub:`Espeon day, Umbreon night. The rest are stones, moss, ice, or affection.`,why:`Vaporeon, Jolteon, and Flareon are the Kanto stone trio, Espeon and Umbreon are Johto friendship, Leafeon and Glaceon came in Gen 4, and Sylveon is the Fairy evolution from X and Y. That is eight evolutions, not nine.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`eevee`,`espeon`,`umbreon`,`sylveon`]},{id:`game-game-freak`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Game Freak develops the main-series games. Satoshi Tajiri founded the studio`,stat:`GF`,sub:`Ken Sugimori drew the first 151. Not a cryptocurrency story.`,why:`Satoshi Tajiri founded Game Freak and created Pokémon, with Red and Green launching in Japan on February 27, 1996. Ken Sugimori illustrated the original 151, and Ash's Japanese name Satoshi nods to Tajiri.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`]},{id:`game-vgc`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`VGC is the official video-game championship circuit`,stat:`VGC`,sub:`Console battles. Worlds also runs a TCG bracket. Different skill.`,why:`The official Video Game Championships are double battles on a console, and Pokémon Champions became the 2026 platform for that circuit. UNITE is a MOBA. The TCG World Championships are a different event under the same weekend umbrella.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`game-wonder-trade`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Wonder Trade sends one Pokémon to a random player`,stat:`WONDER`,sub:`Named Surprise Trade in later games. Not the GTS search.`,why:`Wonder Trade, later Surprise Trade, swaps a party Pokémon with a random trainer online. The Global Trade Station lets you search for a specific species. Two different online tools. Both are games, not card shops.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`game-contest`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Contests are Hoenn and Sinnoh shows, not gym battles`,stat:`CONTEST`,sub:`Cool, Beauty, Cute, Smart, Tough. Super Contests on DS.`,why:`Ruby and Sapphire introduced Pokémon Contests, judged on condition and appeal moves. Diamond and Pearl upgraded them to Super Contests. They are in-game performances. They are not VGC and not a TCG tournament.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`sinnoh`]},{id:`game-sos`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sun and Moon SOS battles let a wild Pokémon call an ally`,stat:`SOS`,sub:`Chaining calls was a shiny and Hidden Ability hunt.`,why:`In Alola, a wild Pokémon can call for help. Long SOS chains were how players hunted shinies and Hidden Abilities in Sun and Moon. Paldea uses outbreaks and sandwiches instead. Different games, different hunt tools.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`,`shiny`]},{id:`game-following-pla`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Arceus lets a caught Pokémon walk beside you in the field`,stat:`FOLLOW`,sub:`Any caught species can come out. Yellow started this with Pikachu.`,why:`Hisui lets you send a caught Pokémon out to walk, scare herds, or battle. HeartGold and SoulSilver did a similar follower. Scarlet and Violet added Let's Go-style auto-battling later. Followers are a field feature.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`switch`]},{id:`game-crystal-infrared`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Crystal used the Game Boy Color infrared port for Mystery Gift`,stat:`IR`,sub:`Two Crystals face to face. Gold and Silver used a cable gift instead.`,why:`Pokémon Crystal can exchange Mystery Gifts over the Game Boy Color infrared window. Gold and Silver used a different Mystery Gift method. It is a 2000 handheld trick, not a modern app.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`,`crystal`]},{id:`game-deoxys-frlg`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Deoxys in FireRed and LeafGreen needed an Aurora Ticket event`,stat:`AURORA`,sub:`Birth Island. The 2026 Switch ports grant that ticket after the league.`,why:`On GBA, Deoxys was an event on Birth Island via the Aurora Ticket. Nintendo said Switch FireRed and LeafGreen players who enter the Hall of Fame receive the Aurora Ticket and a Mystic Ticket for Lugia and Ho-Oh. Those are in-game key items, not TCG tickets.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gba`,`switch`,`frlg`]},{id:`game-jirachi-colosseum`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`A Colosseum bonus disc could send Jirachi into Ruby or Sapphire`,stat:`DISC`,sub:`GameCube. Event Mythical. Not a wild Hoenn spawn.`,why:`Some copies of Pokémon Colosseum included a GameCube bonus disc that distributed Jirachi to Ruby or Sapphire. That is how many Western players obtained Jirachi in Generation III. It is a disc event, not a route 110 encounter.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`gc`,`hoenn`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-manaphy-mythical`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Manaphy is Mythical. Its Ranger Egg was the famous legal path into Sinnoh`,stat:`MANAPHY`,sub:`Ends the Diamond and Pearl regional dex at 151.`,why:`Manaphy sits at the end of the Diamond and Pearl Sinnoh dex. The first Ranger game's Egg transfer was the intended way to obtain it. Phione hatches from a Manaphy breed and is a separate species.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`ds`,`spinoff`]},{id:`game-celebi-gs`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Celebi is Johto's Mythical 251, not Crystal's box legendary`,stat:`251`,sub:`Suicune is the Crystal cover. Celebi is the event onion.`,why:`Celebi is National Dex #251 and the Mythical of Generation II. Crystal's box is Suicune. Ilex Forest events and later distributions were how players obtained Celebi. Cover, player, and Mythical are three different roles.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`,`crystal`]},{id:`game-mewtwo-kanto`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Mewtwo is Kanto's cave legendary. Mew is the event Mythical`,stat:`150`,sub:`Cerulean Cave after the league. Not the 151st.`,why:`Mewtwo is #150 and waits in Cerulean Cave after the Pokémon League in the Kanto games. Mew is #151 and was not a standard wild spawn. The first movie is about Mewtwo. The games still treat them as two species.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`mew`]},{id:`game-zygarde-za-name`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Z-A's title points at Zygarde, Kalos's third legendary`,stat:`ZYGARDE`,sub:`Xerneas, Yveltal, Zygarde. The game is Lumiose, not a new region name.`,why:`X and Y's third legendary is Zygarde. Legends Z-A's name is a nod to that Kalos trio and to Lumiose's AZ. The game is still a Lumiose story with Mega Evolution, not a Zygarde-only battler.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`]},{id:`game-koraidon-ride`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Koraidon and Miraidon are Paldea's box legends and your ride`,stat:`RIDE`,sub:`Scarlet past. Violet future. Saddle forms for land, water, and air.`,why:`In Scarlet you ride Koraidon, and in Violet you ride Miraidon, which are also the cover legendaries. Alola had Ride Pokémon as NPCs. Paldea puts the box legend under you.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-zacian-crowned`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Zacian and Zamazenta have Hero and Crowned forms`,stat:`CROWN`,sub:`Rusted Sword or Rusted Shield. Sword and Shield version mascots.`,why:`In Galar, Zacian Crowned Sword and Zamazenta Crowned Shield are the item-held forms used in the climax. Hero of Many Battles is the other form. Those are game forms, not TCG regulation marks.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-necrozma-fusion`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ultra Sun and Ultra Moon let Necrozma fuse with Solgaleo or Lunala`,stat:`FUSE`,sub:`Dusk Mane or Dawn Wings, then Ultra Necrozma in the story.`,why:`USUM's box forms are Dusk Mane Necrozma and Dawn Wings Necrozma, fusions with Solgaleo or Lunala. Ultra Necrozma is the story transformation. Sun and Moon's boxes were the lions themselves.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-calyrex`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Calyrex is the Crown Tundra legendary that bonds with a steed`,stat:`CALYREX`,sub:`Glastrier or Spectrier. Ice Rider or Shadow Rider.`,why:`The Crown Tundra's story is about Calyrex reclaiming Glastrier or Spectrier. The fused Ice Rider and Shadow Rider forms are battle formes. This is Sword and Shield DLC, October 22, 2020, not a new numbered generation.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-ogerpon`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ogerpon is The Teal Mask's ogre of Kitakami`,stat:`MASK`,sub:`Four masks change its Tera type. Not a Paldea gym mascot.`,why:`Ogerpon is the legendary star of The Teal Mask. Its masks change form and Tera type, and the Loyal Three are the opposing set. Kitakami folklore is DLC story, not a TCG mechanic.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-terapagos`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Terapagos is The Indigo Disk's crystal legendary`,stat:`TERA`,sub:`It explains Terastal. Stellar Tera Type in the climax.`,why:`Terapagos sits at the end of The Indigo Disk and is tied to the origin of Terastal energy. Its Stellar form is a battle spectacle. It is a Paldea DLC legendary, not a Galar Dynamax titan.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-pecharunt`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pecharunt is the Mochi Mayhem mythical that closed Generation IX`,stat:`#1025`,sub:`Epilogue after The Indigo Disk. National Dex 1025.`,why:`Pecharunt appears in the Mochi Mayhem epilogue tied to The Indigo Disk. It is National Dex #1025. That number is a game census. It is not a TCG set code.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`dex`,`switch`]},{id:`game-enamorus`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Enamorus is Hisui's fourth Force of Nature, added in Legends Arceus`,stat:`905`,sub:`Landorus, Thundurus, Tornadus, then Enamorus. National 905.`,why:`Legends Arceus added Enamorus as the fourth Forces of Nature member, National Dex #905. The other three came in Black and White. Incarnate and Therian formes exist. This is a Hisui story, not a Paldea Paradox.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`unova`,`dex`]},{id:`game-kubfu`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Kubfu is the Isle of Armor dojo cub that becomes Urshifu`,stat:`KUBFU`,sub:`Single Strike or Rapid Strike, then possible Gigantamax.`,why:`The Isle of Armor's story gives you Kubfu. Tower floors decide whether it evolves into Single Strike or Rapid Strike Urshifu. Gigantamax Urshifu is a further Galar factor. This is 2020 DLC, not a Paldea Titan.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-meltan-go-box`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Let's Go's Mystery Box is the official Meltan generator for GO`,stat:`BOX`,sub:`Transfer a Pokémon from Let's Go into GO, then open the box.`,why:`If you send a Pokémon from Let's Go into Pokémon GO, GO can give a Mystery Box that spawns Meltan. That loop is why Meltan is a GO-origin Mythical. It is not a wild Kanto grass encounter in Red.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`go`,`kanto`,`switch`]},{id:`game-pla-research`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Arceus completes Pokédex pages by tasks, not only by catching one`,stat:`RESEARCH`,sub:`Seen is not done. Catch, use moves, eat, and sleep tasks.`,why:`Hisui research requires multiple tasks per species — catching several, seeing moves, feeding. A single catch does not finish the page. That is different from the classic 'caught the first one' dex tick.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`dex`,`switch`]},{id:`game-sv-paths`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Scarlet and Violet run three story paths you can mix in any order`,stat:`3 PATH`,sub:`Gyms, Titans, Team Star. Area Zero waits until they are done.`,why:`Victory Road is the gyms, Path of Legends is the Titans with Koraidon or Miraidon, and Starfall Street is Team Star. Area Zero is the finale after those paths. Open world means order is yours, not that there is no plot.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-za-royale`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Z-A Royale is Lumiose's rank ladder, fought in city zones`,stat:`ROYALE`,sub:`Promoted through ranks. Not UNITE and not VGC by itself.`,why:`Legends Z-A's Z-A Royale is an in-city battle circuit with ranks. You fight other trainers in Lumiose districts. Pokémon Champions is the later dedicated ranked platform. Do not mix the two ladders.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`,`play`]},{id:`game-home-bank-path`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The legal path from a 2007 DS cart into Scarlet is Bank then HOME`,stat:`PATH`,sub:`Transporter into Bank, Bank into HOME, HOME into Switch games.`,why:`A Diamond cartridge cannot talk to Scarlet directly. Poké Transporter moved DS Pokémon into Bank on 3DS, Bank moves into HOME, and HOME moves into Switch games that will accept the species.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`home`,`ds`,`3ds`,`switch`]},{id:`game-go-home`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon GO can send many species into HOME, then into the games`,stat:`TRANSFER`,sub:`GO stamp stays. Some Mythicals have extra rules.`,why:`Pokémon GO transfers into Pokémon HOME, and from there into compatible main games. A GO origin stamp remains. Meltan, and many wild legendaries, used this pipe. It is not a TCG redemption.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`go`,`home`]},{id:`game-unite-not-vgc`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`UNITE ranks do not qualify you for VGC Worlds`,stat:`MOBA`,sub:`Different rules, different publisher team, different ladder.`,why:`Pokémon UNITE is a 5v5 objective map with Unite moves and scoring goals. VGC is official double battles on a console, on Champions in 2026. Sharing a license is not sharing a circuit.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unite`,`play`]},{id:`game-go-not-main`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon GO is a phone map, not a numbered main-series generation`,stat:`GO`,sub:`No gym-badge Kanto story. Raids and Community Days instead.`,why:`GO uses real-world walking, Gyms as map control, and Raids. It does not replace Sword and Shield or Scarlet and Violet. Community Day is a GO calendar. It is not a Game Freak boxed launch.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`go`,`phone`]},{id:`game-frlg-2004-vs-2026`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`FireRed 2004 is a GBA cart. FireRed 2026 is an eShop port of that remake`,stat:`2 SKU`,sub:`Same Kanto remake family. Twenty-two years apart. Different platforms.`,why:`The 2004 Game Boy Advance FireRed and LeafGreen are physical remakes of Red and Blue. The 2026 Switch versions are digital rereleases of those remakes, not new remakes of the remakes. Price, platform, and online rules differ.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gba`,`switch`,`frlg`]},{id:`game-bdsp-not-platinum`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Brilliant Diamond and Shining Pearl follow Diamond and Pearl, not Platinum`,stat:`DP`,sub:`151 Sinnoh dex. No Distortion World as Platinum had it.`,why:`BDSP remade Diamond and Pearl. They keep the 151-entry Sinnoh dex and do not rebuild Platinum's Distortion World story. Giratina still exists. The remake target was DP, the way ORAS targeted Ruby and Sapphire rather than Emerald.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`switch`]},{id:`game-oras-not-emerald`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Omega Ruby and Alpha Sapphire remake Ruby and Sapphire, not Emerald`,stat:`RS`,sub:`Primal weather pair. Delta Episode is the extra story.`,why:`ORAS are remakes of the 2002 pair. The Delta Episode, Mega Rayquaza, and the looker's side story are the new meat. The Battle Frontier from Emerald did not return in full. Third versions and remakes are different products.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-usum-not-remake`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Ultra Sun and Ultra Moon are enhanced Alola, not a new region`,stat:`ULTRA`,sub:`Same islands, new episodes. Like Emerald, not like FireRed.`,why:`USUM keep Alola's islands and replace or extend story chapters, the way Emerald extended Hoenn. They are not remakes of a prior generation. FireRed remade Kanto. That is a different job.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-b2w2-not-remake`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Black 2 and White 2 are sequels. HeartGold is a remake. Learn the difference`,stat:`SEQUEL`,sub:`New Unova layout two years later. HGSS retold Johto.`,why:`A sequel continues the story with a new map, as Black 2 does. A remake retells an older game, as HeartGold retells Gold. Ultra Sun is an enhanced version. Three words, three products.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`johto`]},{id:`game-lets-go-not-frlg`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Let's Go is a Yellow-style Kanto on Switch. FireRed is the GBA remake family`,stat:`2 KANTO`,sub:`Let's Go 2018. FireRed 2004 and 2026. Different catching rules.`,why:`Let's Go Pikachu and Let's Go Eevee (2018) use overworld GO-like catching and a follower partner. FireRed and LeafGreen use regular grass battles and the Sevii Islands. Both are Kanto. They are not the same game.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`switch`,`gba`]},{id:`game-pla-not-bdsp`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Arceus is Hisui. Brilliant Diamond is modern Sinnoh`,stat:`2 ERAS`,sub:`Same land, different century, different catch rules.`,why:`Hisui is the past and Sinnoh in BDSP is the present, so you cannot treat Hisuian forms as Brilliant Diamond dex filler. Origin Forme Dialga in PLA is not the BDSP box by itself.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`sinnoh`,`switch`]},{id:`game-za-not-xy`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends Z-A is not an X and Y remake`,stat:`LUMIOSE`,sub:`One city, five years later, real-time Megas. XY walked all of Kalos.`,why:`X and Y are 2013 3DS games across Kalos with turn-based battles. Z-A is a 2025 Lumiose game with real-time battles and a Mega revival. Remakes are ORAS and BDSP. Z-A is a Legends game.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`3ds`,`switch`]},{id:`game-champions-not-worlds-tcg`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokémon Champions is a video game. TCG Worlds is a card tournament`,stat:`2 WORLDS`,sub:`Same company umbrella. Different skill, different deck.`,why:`Play! Pokémon World Championships include a video-game bracket and a trading-card bracket. Pokémon Champions is the 2026 video-game client. A TCG Worlds date is not Champions' April 8 launch, and April 8 is not a card set.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`play`,`switch`]},{id:`game-hyperspace-132`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Hyperspace Pokédex in Mega Dimension has 132 entries`,stat:`132`,sub:`DLC list for Pokémon outside Lumiose City.`,why:`Z-A's Mega Dimension DLC adds a 132-entry Hyperspace Pokédex for species found in Hyperspace Lumiose rather than the city streets. The Lumiose dex stays 232. Add lists, do not mash them into one fake 364 without saying so.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`dex`,`switch`]},{id:`game-galar-dlc-dex`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Isle of Armor has 211 dex entries. Crown Tundra has 210`,stat:`DLC`,sub:`Separate lists from Galar's 400. Expansion Pass, 2020.`,why:`The Isle of Armor Pokédex has 211 entries. The Crown Tundra Pokédex has 210. Those are DLC regional lists, not replacements of the 400-entry Galar dex. Count them as extras, the way Kitakami is extra to Paldea.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`dex`,`switch`]},{id:`game-johto-hgss-dex`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`HeartGold and SoulSilver's Johto Pokédex has 256 entries`,stat:`256`,sub:`Expanded from Gold and Silver's original New Pokédex.`,why:`HGSS uses a 256-entry Johto Pokédex, larger than Gold and Silver's original list, because more returning species sit in Johto and Kanto on DS. Celebi still closes the classic 251 National from Gen 2. Different lists.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`dex`,`ds`]},{id:`game-national-890`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sword and Shield's new Galar species closed the National Dex at 890 before DLC`,stat:`890`,sub:`Eternatus is 890. DLC then added Kubfu through Calyrex.`,why:`Base Sword and Shield added Galar species through Eternatus at 890. The Expansion Pass added Kubfu, Urshifu, Zarude, the new Regis, the steeds, and Calyrex through 898. Hisui then pushed to 905.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`dex`]},{id:`game-switch-msrp-59`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Main-series Switch games launched at $59.99 USD through Z-A's Switch edition`,stat:`$59.99`,sub:`Let's Go, SwSh, BDSP, PLA, SV, Z-A Switch. Switch 2 editions are $69.99.`,why:`Nintendo's standard launch MSRP for main-series Switch Pokémon games was $59.99 USD, including Let's Go, Sword and Shield, Brilliant Diamond and Shining Pearl, Legends Arceus, Scarlet and Violet, and Legends Z-A on Switch. Z-A's Switch 2 Edition and Pokopia listed at $69.99. Those are stickers, not solds.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`switch`,`switch2`]},{id:`game-natures-25`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`There are 25 natures. Five of them are neutral`,stat:`25`,sub:`Adamant, Modest, Jolly, Timid are the common competitive picks.`,why:`Ruby and Sapphire introduced 25 natures. Twenty tilt one stat up and one down. Five — Hardy, Docile, Serious, Bashful, Quirky — are neutral. A nature is not an Ability and not a TCG attack name.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`]},{id:`game-ev-510`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`A Pokémon can earn 510 EVs total, 252 in one stat in modern games`,stat:`510`,sub:`Old cap per stat was 255. Modern competitive uses 252.`,why:`Effort values total 510 across all stats. Since later generations the per-stat cap used in play is 252. Vitamins, wings, and Paldea's vitamins still feed this system. Cards do not have EVs.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`game-iv-31`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`A perfect IV is 31 in that stat`,stat:`31`,sub:`Six stats. Hyper Training can max them after a level cap.`,why:`Each of HP, Attack, Defense, Special Attack, Special Defense, and Speed has an IV from 0 to 31. Bottle Caps and Hyper Training in later games can maximize IVs after a level requirement. Breeding still inherits IVs with Destiny Knot.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`game-destiny-knot`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Destiny Knot passes five IVs from the parents when breeding`,stat:`KNOT`,sub:`Held item. Masuda is language. Destiny Knot is IVs.`,why:`If a parent holds Destiny Knot, five of the offspring's IVs come from the parents instead of three. The Masuda method is a different held-nothing rule about language. Power items can pin a specific IV. All of this is the Day Care, not a pack.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`play`,`shiny`]},{id:`game-oval-charm`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Oval Charm makes eggs appear faster`,stat:`OVAL`,sub:`Key Item, usually a dex reward. Not the Shiny Charm.`,why:`The Oval Charm increases the chance the Day Care or picnic will produce an egg. The Shiny Charm increases shiny odds. Two charms, two jobs. Confusing them is how people mis-teach hunts.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`game-hidden-machine`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Hidden Machines were reusable field moves you had to teach someone`,stat:`HM`,sub:`Cut, Fly, Surf, Strength. Alola and Galar stopped requiring them.`,why:`From Gen 1 through Kalos, HMs taught moves that also opened the map and could not be forgotten easily. Alola moved that to Ride Pokémon. Galar and Paldea dropped the system. A TM is a battle tutor item, not an HM.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`]},{id:`game-tm-infinite`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`From Sun and Moon onward, TMs are not consumed on use`,stat:`TM`,sub:`Older games ate the disc. Later games let you reuse it.`,why:`In Generations I through VI, a TM was usually a one-time disc. Sun and Moon made TMs reusable. Scarlet and Violet craft TMs from materials. That is a game-item rule, not a card rarity.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`]},{id:`game-pokerus-not-pokerus-tcg`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Pokérus is a Gen 2 battle-stat virus, not a trading-card status`,stat:`PKRS`,sub:`Doubles EVs. Spreads in the party. Harmless.`,why:`Pokérus has existed since Gold and Silver as a rare status that boosts EV gain. It is not printed as a TCG mechanic. If a card mentions a virus, that is paper. If a DS summary screen shows PKRS, that is the game.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`play`]},{id:`game-fairy-steel-nerf`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Generation VI made Steel stop resisting Dark and Ghost`,stat:`NERF`,sub:`Fairy arrives. Steel stays strong against Fairy and Poison.`,why:`When X and Y added Fairy, Steel lost its resistances to Dark and Ghost. Steel still resists Fairy and Poison. Type charts are game rules that have changed. The TCG has its own weakness chart.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-gen1-special`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Generation I had a single Special stat, not Special Attack and Special Defense`,stat:`SPEC`,sub:`Yellow still uses one Special. Gold split it in two.`,why:`Red, Blue, and Yellow share one Special stat for both attacking and defending with special types. Gold and Silver split it into Special Attack and Special Defense. Stadium and later games honor the split when they can.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gb`]},{id:`game-crit-gen1`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Generation I critical hits used the Speed stat, which broke the meta`,stat:`CRIT`,sub:`Fast Pokémon crit more. Gen 2 changed that rule.`,why:`In Red and Blue, a Pokémon's Speed fed its critical-hit rate, so Persian and Alakazam crit constantly. Gold and Silver divorced crits from Speed. That is why Stadium's rule set and later games feel different.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`gb`,`play`]},{id:`game-truant`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Slaking's Truant ability forces it to loaf every other turn`,stat:`TRUANT`,sub:`Hoenn. Huge stats, built-in rest. An Ability, not a status.`,why:`Slaking has enormous base stats and the Ability Truant, so it skips every second turn. Giga Impact's recharge is a move, while Truant is an Ability, and those live in different boxes on the summary screen.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`]},{id:`game-sturdy`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sturdy lets a Pokémon survive a would-be KO from full HP`,stat:`STURDY`,sub:`Like a Focus Sash baked into the species. Ability, Gen 3 onward.`,why:`Sturdy, from Ruby and Sapphire, leaves the Pokémon at 1 HP if a hit would have KO'd it from full health (after later-generation wording). Focus Sash is an item that does a similar job once. Abilities and items are different.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`play`]},{id:`game-intimidate`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Intimidate lowers the opponent's Attack when the Pokémon enters`,stat:`INTIM`,sub:`Ability since Ruby. Gyarados, Arcanine, Salamence.`,why:`Intimidate is a Generation III Ability that drops the foe's Attack on switch-in. It is one of the most important competitive traits ever printed in the games. The TCG has its own attacks named Intimidate. Do not mix the rules.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`play`]},{id:`game-levitate`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Levitate grants immunity to Ground, including Earthquake`,stat:`FLY`,sub:`Ability. Gengar, Misdreavus, most pure Ghosts that float.`,why:`Levitate is an Ability that makes the Pokémon immune to Ground-type moves. Flying type and the Air Balloon item can look similar and are not the same. Gengar has Levitate in older games and later gained a different ability in some generations.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`play`]},{id:`game-weather-hoenn`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Hoenn popularized weather teams: sun, rain, sand, hail`,stat:`WEATHER`,sub:`Drought, Drizzle, Sand Stream, later Snow Warning.`,why:`Ruby and Sapphire's drought, drizzle, and sand abilities, plus hail, created weather teams. ORAS Primals lock sun or rain. Paldea still uses weather, plus terrains from later gens. Weather is a field condition, not a TCG stadium by default.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`play`]},{id:`game-trick-room`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Trick Room makes slower Pokémon move first for four turns`,stat:`TR`,sub:`Psychic move from Diamond and Pearl. Competitive staple.`,why:`Trick Room, introduced in Generation IV, reverses Speed order so the slowest unmoved Pokémon acts first. It lasts four turns including the turn it is used. It is a game move. It is not a card stadium unless a card says so.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`play`]},{id:`game-terrain`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Terrains — Electric, Grassy, Misty, Psychic — started in X and Y and grew in Alola`,stat:`TERRAIN`,sub:`Field underfoot. Not weather. Not Terastal.`,why:`X and Y introduced Electric, Grassy, and Misty Terrain. Sun and Moon added Psychic Terrain and terrain-extending abilities. Terrains are ground-level field effects. They are not Dynamax weather and not a Tera type.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`alola`,`play`]},{id:`game-mega-rayquaza`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Mega Rayquaza does not need a Mega Stone, it needs Dragon Ascent`,stat:`RAY`,sub:`ORAS Delta Episode. It also disables weather Primals.`,why:`Omega Ruby and Alpha Sapphire's Mega Rayquaza Mega Evolves if it knows Dragon Ascent, with no Mega Stone. That exception is famous in competitive play because it can still hold a different item. It is an ORAS rule, not a Z-A invention.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`3ds`,`mega`,`play`]},{id:`game-bond-greninja`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Battle Bond Greninja is a special Ash-Greninja form from X and Y events`,stat:`BOND`,sub:`Not a Mega Stone. A different Ability that changes form after a KO.`,why:`The Battle Bond Ability lets a special Greninja change into Ash-Greninja after it causes a KO. It came from X and Y distributions tied to the anime. It is not Greninja's standard Mega, and Z-A Megas are a different list.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-zygarde-cells`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sun and Moon let you collect Zygarde Cells to build 10%, 50%, and Complete forms`,stat:`CELL`,sub:`Alola. Core and Cell items. Not a Kalos overworld catch-all.`,why:`In Sun and Moon you collect Zygarde Cells and Cores to assemble forms. 10% Zygarde, 50% Zygarde, and Complete Zygarde are those assemblies. X and Y had a 50% Zygarde encounter. Different games, different methods.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`kalos`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-rotom-dex`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Sun and Moon's Pokédex is possessed by Rotom`,stat:`ROTOM`,sub:`Talking dex. Later games used Rotom Phones.`,why:`Alola's Pokédex is a Rotom Dex that talks, maps, and sometimes jokes. Sword and Shield and Scarlet and Violet put Rotom in your phone instead. It is a UI character, not a box legendary.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`alola`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-c-gear`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Black and White's C-Gear is the bottom-screen wireless hub`,stat:`C-GEAR`,sub:`Unova. Pass Powers. Infrared. Not a Pokégear.`,why:`The C-Gear in Black and White handles wireless features and Pass Powers on the DS touch screen. Johto's Pokégear was a phone-map-radio and Sinnoh's Pokétch was a watch, so each generation's gadget has its own name.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`ds`]},{id:`game-pokegear`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Gold and Silver's Pokégear is a phone, map, and radio`,stat:`GEAR`,sub:`Call trainers to rematch. Radio tunes for certain encounters.`,why:`The Pokégear is Generation II's multi-tool: map, phone, radio. Certain radio stations boost encounter types. Later gadgets replaced it. It is not the Pokétch and not the Rotom Phone.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`gbc`]},{id:`game-poketch`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Diamond and Pearl's Pokétch is a wrist gadget with apps`,stat:`WATCH`,sub:`Calculator, counter, coin toss. Bottom screen on DS.`,why:`The Pokétch in Sinnoh is a digital watch you fill with small apps, and BDSP brought it back. It is a Pokédex companion, not the dex itself. Johto used a Pokégear and Unova used a C-Gear.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`ds`]},{id:`game-vs-seeker`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`FireRed and LeafGreen's Vs. Seeker lets you rematch trainers on a route`,stat:`SEEK`,sub:`Battery item. Recharges. Not a radar for legendaries.`,why:`The Vs. Seeker is a Key Item in FireRed, LeafGreen, Diamond, Pearl, and Platinum that finds rematches on routes you already cleared. It is for grinding, not for tracking roaming beasts. Those have their own mechanics.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`sinnoh`,`gba`]},{id:`game-roaming`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Roaming legendaries move to another route when you enter theirs`,stat:`ROAM`,sub:`Raikou, Entei, Latios, Latias, the Kanto birds in some games.`,why:`Gold and Silver's Raikou and Entei flee to a new route when you land on theirs. Later games reused roaming for Latios, Latias, and some birds. A static cave legendary like Mewtwo does not roam. Two hunt types.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`johto`,`hoenn`]},{id:`game-static-legend`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Static legendaries wait in a cave or chamber until you interact`,stat:`STATIC`,sub:`Mewtwo, Groudon, Dialga. One shot unless you reset or use a later trick.`,why:`A static legendary stands in one place, often after a plot flag. Save in front, or you may have to wait for a later reset method. Roaming beasts are the ones that run. Box covers are often static story fights.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`]},{id:`game-master-ball`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Master Ball catches without fail. You usually get one per game`,stat:`MASTER`,sub:`Team Rocket's prize in Kanto. Later games hide it in a story lab.`,why:`The Master Ball is a 100 percent catch item, and Kanto gives one after Giovanni or Silph. Later games still ration them. It is a game item, not a guaranteed TCG pull.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`]},{id:`game-safari`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Safari Zone is a park where you cannot fight, only throw bait and balls`,stat:`SAFARI`,sub:`Kanto, Hoenn, Johto HGSS. Sinnoh used the Great Marsh instead.`,why:`Safari Zones limit you to Safari Balls and bait or rocks, with a step count. Kanto's Fuchsia Safari Zone is the original, Hoenn has one, and Sinnoh's Great Marsh is the cousin.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`hoenn`]},{id:`game-grand-underground`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Brilliant Diamond and Shining Pearl's Grand Underground is a digging and hideaway layer`,stat:`UNDER`,sub:`Statues, balls, Pokémon hideaways. Not Platinum's Distortion World.`,why:`BDSP expands Diamond and Pearl's underground into the Grand Underground, with Pokémon Hideaways that let you catch species outside the old 151 overworld. It is a remake sandbox, not Hisui.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`switch`]},{id:`game-area-zero`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Area Zero is Paldea's crater finale, under the Great Crater`,stat:`ZERO`,sub:`Professor's lab. Paradox Pokémon. Terapagos deeper in DLC.`,why:`Area Zero sits in the middle of Paldea. The main game's end happens there with the professor and Koraidon or Miraidon. The Indigo Disk returns you deeper for Terapagos. It is a location, not a TCG subset name.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-team-star`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Team Star are Paldea dropouts in parked buses, not Team Rocket`,stat:`STAR`,sub:`Five squads. Starfall Street path. Cassiopeia is the twist.`,why:`Team Star is Scarlet and Violet's antagonist gang of ex-students in makeshift bases. Team Rocket, Magma, Aqua, Galactic, Plasma, Flare, Skull, and Yell are other games' teams. Do not file Star as Rocket.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`]},{id:`game-team-yell`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Team Yell are Shield-fan hooligans in Galar, not villains who steal Pokémon`,stat:`YELL`,sub:`They cheer Marnie. Different job from Rocket or Plasma.`,why:`Team Yell exists to heckle and hype Marnie's gym challenge, so they are pests rather than legend-thieves. Team Rocket steals, Team Plasma preaches liberation, and Team Flare wants a beautiful apocalypse.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`]},{id:`game-team-flare`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Team Flare is Kalos's glam villain team that fires the ultimate weapon`,stat:`FLARE`,sub:`Lysandre. X and Y. Z-A still lives with that leftover.`,why:`Team Flare, led by Lysandre, is the X and Y antagonist group. Their ultimate weapon is why Lumiose still has scars in Legends Z-A. That is Kalos plot, not a Galar gym theme.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`3ds`]},{id:`game-team-plasma`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Team Plasma wants to separate people and Pokémon in Unova`,stat:`PLASMA`,sub:`N. Ghetsis. Black and White. A different sermon in Black 2.`,why:`Team Plasma is Unova's villain team, with N as the king who battles you using legendary dragons and Ghetsis as the actual architect. Black 2 and White 2 show Plasma two years later, split into factions.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`ds`]},{id:`game-team-galactic`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Team Galactic is Sinnoh's space-themed villain team`,stat:`GALACTIC`,sub:`Cyrus. Spear Pillar. Dialga or Palkia. Giratina objects.`,why:`Team Galactic, led by Cyrus, tries to reset the universe with Dialga or Palkia at Spear Pillar. Platinum makes Giratina drag him into the Distortion World. That is Sinnoh. Hisui's Galaxy Team is a historical survey corps, a different organization.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`ds`]},{id:`game-galaxy-team`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`The Galaxy Team in Hisui is a survey corps, not Team Galactic`,stat:`GALAXY`,sub:`Jubilife Village. Professor Laventon. You join as a recruit.`,why:`In Legends Arceus the Galaxy Team maps Hisui. Team Galactic in Diamond and Pearl is a villain team centuries later with a similar naming wink. Do not teach kids that Cyrus runs the Hisui campus.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`sinnoh`,`switch`]},{id:`game-team-rocket`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Team Rocket is Kanto and Johto's Pokémon-theft gang`,stat:`ROCKET`,sub:`Giovanni. Jessie and James are the anime pair. Games have the hideouts.`,why:`Team Rocket, led by Giovanni, is the first villain team, in Kanto, then a leftover in Johto. Hideouts, the Game Corner, and Silph Co. are the game beats. The anime pair Jessie and James are not the only Rocket members.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`johto`]},{id:`game-silph`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Silph Co. in Saffron City is the Kanto office Team Rocket occupies`,stat:`SILPH`,sub:`Master Ball origin in Gen 1. Lapras gift on the upper floors.`,why:`Silph Company is a skyscraper dungeon in Saffron that Team Rocket occupies in Red, Blue, Yellow, and the remakes. The Master Ball and a Lapras are the famous prizes. It is a Kanto building, not a Hoenn Devon office.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`]},{id:`game-devon`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Devon Corporation is Hoenn's Silph-like company in Rustboro`,stat:`DEVON`,sub:`Mr. Stone. Devon Scope. The goods you fetch in the early game.`,why:`Devon Corporation is Hoenn's tech company. Early Ruby and Sapphire plot has you recover Devon Goods. The Devon Scope later lets you see invisible Kecleon. It is not Silph and not Quasartico.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`]},{id:`game-quasartico`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Quasartico Inc. redevelops Lumiose in Legends Z-A`,stat:`CORP`,sub:`Urban plan. Team MZ works with them. AZ's city is being rebuilt.`,why:`Quasartico Inc. is the company driving Lumiose's redevelopment in Legends Z-A. It is a 2025 Kalos plot device, not Devon, not Silph, not Team Galactic. Urbain or Taunie later ties into its leadership in the story.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`]},{id:`game-az-hotel`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`AZ owns Hotel Z and is the thousand-year king from X and Y`,stat:`AZ`,sub:`Floette. Ultimate weapon. He is not a box legendary.`,why:`AZ is the ancient king who built the ultimate weapon in Kalos lore. In X and Y you meet him late. In Z-A he runs Hotel Z and sponsors Team MZ. He is a human NPC with a Floette, not the cover monster.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`]},{id:`game-team-mz`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Team MZ is your Lumiose crew in Legends Z-A, based at Hotel Z`,stat:`MZ`,sub:`Urbain or Taunie leads. Naveen and Lida are members. You join.`,why:`Team MZ is not a villain team. It is the player-aligned group at Hotel Z trying to protect Lumiose. The name nods to Mega Evolution and to AZ. Team Flare is the old Kalos villain crew.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`switch`]},{id:`game-n-unova`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`N is the Unova rival who battles with a legendary dragon`,stat:`N`,sub:`Friend and foil. Ghetsis is the actual villain.`,why:`N, raised by Team Plasma, is the Black and White rival who uses Reshiram or Zekrom opposite yours. He is not Ghetsis. He is not the box art by himself, even though his dragon is. Later games still treat him as that character.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`ds`]},{id:`game-cygnus-not`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Cynthia is the Sinnoh Champion, famous for the Garchomp closer`,stat:`CHAMP`,sub:`Celestic. Spear Pillar help. She is not the box legendary.`,why:`Cynthia is Sinnoh's Champion. Her Garchomp is the meme closer. She also appears in later games as a strong NPC. Champions the 2026 video game is a different product that borrowed the English word.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`sinnoh`,`play`]},{id:`game-lance`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Lance is the Dragon-type Elite Four member, later Champion in Johto`,stat:`LANCE`,sub:`Kanto Elite Four. Johto Champion. Same clan as Clair.`,why:`Lance appears in Generation I as an Elite Four dragon specialist and in Generation II as Champion. Clair is his cousin in Blackthorn. He is a trainer. He is not Dragonite on the box.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`,`johto`]},{id:`game-steven`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Steven Stone is Hoenn's Champion, a Steel collector`,stat:`STEVEN`,sub:`Devon heir. Metagross. Wallace takes the title in Emerald.`,why:`Steven is Champion in Ruby and Sapphire and ORAS. Emerald makes Wallace the Champion and Steven a post-game fight. Same region, third version, different title holder. That is why Emerald is not just a skin.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hoenn`,`play`]},{id:`game-diantha`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Diantha is the Kalos Champion, a movie star with Gardevoir`,stat:`DIANTHA`,sub:`X and Y. Mega Gardevoir. Ansha in Z-A DLC is her daughter.`,why:`Diantha is Kalos's Champion in X and Y. Mega Dimension's Ansha is presented as her daughter hunting a Rayquaza gift. That is a 2025 DLC beat. Diantha is not Serena, and not the Xerneas on the box.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kalos`,`play`]},{id:`game-alder`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Alder is Unova's Champion in Black and White, with a Volcarona`,stat:`ALDER`,sub:`Iris becomes Champion in Black 2 and White 2.`,why:`Alder is the wandering Champion of Black and White. Black 2 and White 2 make Iris the Champion instead, another sequel change. Unova's Champion title moves. The box dragons stay Reshiram, Zekrom, and Kyurem.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`unova`,`play`]},{id:`game-leon`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Leon is Galar's Champion who never lost until you`,stat:`LEON`,sub:`Charizard. The cup. Hop's brother. Not Zacian.`,why:`Leon is the undefeated Galar Champion in Sword and Shield, with a Charizard that can Gigantamax. Hop is his brother and your rival. Zacian and Zamazenta remain the box legendaries.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`galar`,`switch`,`play`]},{id:`game-geeta`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Geeta is Paldea's Top Champion at the Pokémon League`,stat:`GEETA`,sub:`Naranja or Uva League. Glimmora. Not Koraidon.`,why:`Geeta is the Paldea League's Top Champion in Scarlet and Violet. Nemona is the rival who already has the champion rank as a student. Two champion-titled NPCs, one box legendary pair. Keep them straight.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`paldea`,`switch`,`play`]},{id:`game-must-trade-version`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Version exclusives are why the pair exists: you trade, or you miss them`,stat:`PAIR`,sub:`Oddish or Bellsprout. Groudon or Kyogre. Past or future Paradox.`,why:`From Red and Green onward, each paired version holds Pokémon the other does not. That is the trade hook. Later games added version-exclusive legendaries and Paradox sets. A single SKU does not hold the full wild list.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`kanto`]},{id:`game-third-version`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`A third version is an enhanced retell, not a remake of a remake`,stat:`THIRD`,sub:`Yellow, Crystal, Emerald, Platinum. USUM is the Alola cousin.`,why:`Yellow, Crystal, Emerald, and Platinum are third versions that expand a pair. They add a new cover legendary and quality-of-life. Remakes (FireRed, HGSS, ORAS, BDSP) retell an older generation on new hardware. Sequels (Black 2) continue the story.`,tags:[`game`,`fact`]},{id:`game-legends-line`,kicker:`Video game`,headline:`Legends games are main-series-adjacent stories in one place, not gym rambles`,stat:`LEGENDS`,sub:`Hisui in 2022. Lumiose in 2025. Open catch, then a new battle feel.`,why:`Pokémon Legends: Arceus and Pokémon Legends: Z-A are Game Freak games set in a slice of a known region with different catch and battle rules. They count as main-family, not as Ranger. They are not the numbered pair for their generation (Sword and Shield, Scarlet and Violet).`,tags:[`game`,`fact`,`hisui`,`kalos`,`switch`]}],i={note:e,asOf:t,count:314,facts:r};export{t as asOf,n as count,i as default,r as facts,e as note};